Consider a simple absolute value inequality, as follows:

$$
\mid x \mid < 7
$$

This inequality specifies every number $x$ that is less than a distance of $7$ from $0$. So, it represents the same thing as the following compound inequality:

$$
-7 < x < 7
$$

To solve an absolute value inequality, we first convert it to a compound inequality to remove the absolute value. Then, we can solve the compound inequality using the usual methods.

To illustrate, let's solve the following inequality:

$$
\mid 2x+3 \mid \leq 5
$$

First, we interpret the inequality: the inequality specifies every number $2x+3$ whose distance from zero is less than or equal to $5$. So, it represents the same thing as the following compound inequality:

$$
-5 \leq 2x+3 \leq 5
$$

Now, we can solve the compound inequality using the usual methods:

$$
-5 \le 2x + 3 \le 5; - 8 \le 2x \le 2; - 4 \le x \le 1
$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "53ecd5cc-486a-584d-abc8-f75ec26f12cc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8825 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e46e2f60-2005-54d4-8f5c-97b81542dd48" :step/math-academy-id 145541 :step/next {:db/id 17592186069605}} {:db/id 17592186069605 :step/content {:db/id 17592186068717 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186068491 :question/id #uuid "60c2e025-0b7e-5ac2-afec-adeb5f65eed1" :question/math-academy-id "e-14222"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e107f878-5b22-54bf-90fc-b8ea8a22c77d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id 17592186048267} {:db/id 17592186050734}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186069130} {:db/id 17592186069131} {:db/id 17592186069289}] :knowledge-point/title "Absolute Value Inequalities Containing a Less Than (or Equal To) Symbol"} :step/id #uuid "461b27d5-3e08-5e71-9e57-464e06a7b559" :step/math-academy-id 145542 :step/next {:db/id 17592186069606}} {:db/id 17592186069606 :step/content {:db/id 17592186068718 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186068510 :question/id #uuid "12d8aea9-62dd-5181-bd6e-54c11eba4152" :question/math-academy-id "e-14708"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "65964b91-8f0e-5ffc-97d3-e5b87a6bade5" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186069132} {:db/id 17592186069133} {:db/id 17592186069294}] :knowledge-point/title "Cases Where the Coefficient in the Absolute Value Is Negative"} :step/id #uuid "95df2774-fa6c-5c44-85c3-1203c060ec1a" :step/math-academy-id 145546 :step/next {:db/id 17592186069607}} {:db/id 17592186069607 :step/content {:db/id 17592186070027 :tutorial/content "We take a similar approach for absolute value inequalities involving the "greater than" symbol. To illustrate, consider the following absolute value inequality:

