$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "53ecd5cc-486a-584d-abc8-f75ec26f12cc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8825 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id 17592186069605 :step/content {:db/id 17592186068717 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186068491 :question/id #uuid "60c2e025-0b7e-5ac2-afec-adeb5f65eed1" :question/math-academy-id "e-14222"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e107f878-5b22-54bf-90fc-b8ea8a22c77d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id 17592186048267} {:db/id 17592186050734}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186069130} {:db/id 17592186069131} {:db/id 17592186069289}] :knowledge-point/title "Absolute Value Inequalities Containing a Less Than (or Equal To) Symbol"} :step/id #uuid "461b27d5-3e08-5e71-9e57-464e06a7b559" :step/math-academy-id 145542 :step/next {:db/id 17592186069606}} {:db/id 17592186069606 :step/content {:db/id 17592186068718 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186068510 :question/id #uuid "12d8aea9-62dd-5181-bd6e-54c11eba4152" :question/math-academy-id "e-14708"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "65964b91-8f0e-5ffc-97d3-e5b87a6bade5" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186069132} {:db/id 17592186069133} {:db/id 17592186069294}] :knowledge-point/title "Cases Where the Coefficient in the Absolute Value Is Negative"} :step/id #uuid "95df2774-fa6c-5c44-85c3-1203c060ec1a" :step/math-academy-id 145546 :step/next {:db/id 17592186069607}} {:db/id 17592186069607 :step/content {:db/id 17592186070027 :tutorial/content "We take a similar approach for absolute value inequalities involving the "greater than" symbol. To illustrate, consider the following absolute value inequality:

$$
\mid x \mid \geq 6
$$

To solve this absolute value inequality, we convert it into a compound inequality to remove the absolute value. The inequality specifies every number $x$ whose distance from zero is greater than or equal to $6$. This corresponds to the following compound inequality:

$$
x\begin{vmatrix}\le - 6 & or & x\end{vmatrix}\ge 6
$$

We use the word "or" instead of "and" since only one of the inequalities

$$
x\leq-6
$$

or

$$
x\geq 6
$$

needs to be true for

$$
\mid x \mid \geq 6
$$

to be true." :tutorial/id #uuid "93e8452a-53ea-5e58-807a-00e95af99af1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8830 :tutorial/title "Greater Than (or Equal To) Absolute Value Inequalities"} :step/id #uuid "3706f0b6-4695-54b2-974b-2b4f77638739" :step/math-academy-id 145550 :step/next {:db/id 17592186069608}} {:db/id 17592186070027 :tutorial/content "We take a similar approach for absolute value inequalities involving the "greater than" symbol. To illustrate, consider the following absolute value inequality:

