> A calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true} {:db/id 17592186101020 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186100784 :question/id #uuid "a63d3bdb-ca20-51f5-b876-7ec8950a46dd" :question/math-academy-id "e-7574"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d2b1036-0ea4-5fd1-bfed-885dcbae618b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186101113 :question/id #uuid "284fac66-d855-554d-969c-bb28dda1de3d" :question/math-academy-id "q-119091"} {:db/id 17592186101196 :question/id #uuid "be45f13d-84b8-5eb7-9480-670af18ba1fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-15331"} {:db/id 17592186101285 :question/id #uuid "9ce65a44-c25d-58ff-9d3a-64a6416853bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-18673"} {:db/id 17592186101378 :question/id #uuid "2985ef6b-208a-5435-84a9-964c5b807629" :question/math-academy-id "q-316191"} {:db/id 17592186101482 :question/id #uuid "58c86b66-19dd-5bfc-854b-6c90750a2a5a" :question/math-academy-id "q-51356"}] :knowledge-point/title "Maximizing a Profit Function by Constructing a Revenue and Cost Function"} {:db/id 17592186100784 :question/id #uuid "a63d3bdb-ca20-51f5-b876-7ec8950a46dd" :question/math-academy-id "e-7574" :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id 17592186101020 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d2b1036-0ea4-5fd1-bfed-885dcbae618b" :knowledge-point/title "Maximizing a Profit Function by Constructing a Revenue and Cost Function"}] :question/problem "A hotel has $21$ rooms. The manager has observed that when the room rate is $\[math]12$ per night, all rooms are occupied and, for every $\[/math]1$ increase in the rate, a single room is vacated. If the maintenance of each rented room is $\[math]3$ per night, what rate per room should the manager set to obtain the maximum profit, and what is the maximum profit?" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.

First, note that if there are $x$ *vacant* rooms, the hotel has $(21-x)$ occupied rooms, and the rate per room is $(12+x)$ dollars. So, the total revenue (in dollars) is given by

$$
\begin{aligned}
R(x) &= (21 - x)_(⏟)_(occupied rooms) \cdot (12 + x)_(⏟)_(rate per room) \\
&= 252 + 21x - 12x - x^{2} \\
&= 252 + 9x - x^{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

On the other hand, for having $(21-x)$ rooms rented out, the hotel has a cost (in dollars) of

$$
\begin{aligned}
C(x) &= 3(21 - x) \\
&= 63 - 3x
\end{aligned}
$$

Therefore, the profit function is given by

$$
\begin{aligned}
P(x) &= R(x) - C(x) \\
P(x) &= (252 + 9x - x^{2}) - (63 - 3x) \\
&= 189 + 12x - x^{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

Since there are $21$ rooms, the permitted domain is

$$
0 \leq x\leq 21
$$

Therefore, $x=0$ and $x=21$ are candidates for the global maxima of $P(x)$.

We need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $0 \lt x\lt 21$. Taking the derivative, we get

$$
P'(x) = 12 - 2x \,
$$

and solving $P'(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:

$$
\begin{aligned}
P^{′}(x) &= 0 \\
12 - 2x &= 0 \\
2x &= 12 \\
x &= 6
\end{aligned}
$$

Notice that $x=6$ lies in the interval $0 < x < 21$. All that's left now is to confirm that $x=6$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(x)$. To do this, we compute the second derivative:

$$
P''(x) = -2 < 0
$$

Since the second derivative is negative, the point $x=6$ is indeed a local maximum of $P(x)$. And since $P(x)$ is quadratic, we conclude that $x=6$ is a global maximum of $P(x)$.

Therefore, we conclude that the hotel will have a maximum profit when there are $x=6$ vacant rooms.

We know that the rate per room is $(12+x)$ dollars. If there are $6$ vacant rooms, then the rate per room is

$$
\begin{aligned}
12 + 6 &= \[math]18
\end{aligned}
$$

Therefore, the manager should charge a room rate of $\[/math]18$ per night."} {:db/id 17592186101113 :question/id #uuid "284fac66-d855-554d-969c-bb28dda1de3d" :question/math-academy-id "q-119091" :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186101020 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d2b1036-0ea4-5fd1-bfed-885dcbae618b" :knowledge-point/title "Maximizing a Profit Function by Constructing a Revenue and Cost Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c7d727ea-08a3-55dd-a017-4d107ec1aa2f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100" :db/id 17592186098014} {:answer/id #uuid "e882f15d-cbb3-58de-8db6-593afd851087" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "38" :db/id 17592186098015} {:answer/id #uuid "4a9cdd31-c47f-5335-b161-947bd2eae8c6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "200" :db/id 17592186098016} {:answer/id #uuid "51adfce4-2819-5700-8925-9a7b4a6d258d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id 17592186098017} {:answer/id #uuid "5755991d-f45e-58ee-b0da-3896a698d7fe" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id 17592186098018}] :answer-field/id #uuid "9c185337-13a1-5e13-a931-af896063703c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186098013}] :question/problem "A summer camp has capacity for $200$ kids. The price for each kid attending the camp is $$300$. The cost of setting up the camp is made up of a fixed cost of $$2000$ plus a cost of $$100$ per kid. How many kids should attend the summer camp to maximize its profit?

