8} :answer/value "\frac{1}{5}" :db/id 17592186063355} {:answer/id #uuid "8cc67312-c83d-5cab-b917-ee2161d29129" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\frac{1}{3}" :db/id 17592186063356} {:answer/id #uuid "c283d4fd-1d67-5c92-a428-3fc3d98830e4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\frac{1}{4}" :db/id 17592186063357} {:answer/id #uuid "cad706a5-f7c0-5286-9b96-bf0ba4a525c4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id 17592186063358} {:answer/id #uuid "de134615-356c-50aa-8b5e-53d741c02ca0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id 17592186063359}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id 17592186063356} :answer-field/id #uuid "5881a3d1-5740-518f-bfdd-3ac99c905e6b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493} :db/id 17592186063354}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487} :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=4x-\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and the point $P(0,0)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$  calculate ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(0).$" :question/worked-solution "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(0,0).$

$$
\begin{aligned}f(x) & =4x-\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\ {f}^{′}(x) & =4-\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\ {f}^{′}(0) & =3\end{aligned}
$$

Since $(0,0)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(0,0)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$

Furthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,

$$
\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(0) & =\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\  & =\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(0)} \\  & =\frac{1}{3}.\end{aligned}
