id "q-342582" :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186064252 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e05385-7cb6-5f21-b0d8-a7641d912d5b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d41e761f-f00d-5ff2-b6d3-4945c96a48d4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\frac{1}{8}" :db/id 17592186316308}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d41e761f-f00d-5ff2-b6d3-4945c96a48d4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\frac{1}{8}" :db/id 17592186316308} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc60e108-fe27-56bc-ab5b-e81061889adb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id 17592186317072}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)={e}^{2x}$ and the point $P(\operatorname{ln}⁡2,4)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ calculate ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(4).$

${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(4)=$
{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(\operatorname{ln}⁡2,4).$

$$
\begin{aligned}\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{2x} \\ {f}^{′}(x) & =2{e}^{2x} \\ {f}^{′}(\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =2{e}^{2\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\ {f}^{′}(\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =2{e}^{\operatorname{ln}⁡4} \\ {f}^{′}(\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =2\cdot 4 \\ {f}^{′}(\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =8.\end{aligned}\end{aligned}
$$

Since $(\operatorname{ln}⁡2,4)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(4,\operatorname{ln}⁡2)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$

Furthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,

$$
\begin{aligned}\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(4) & =\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))} \\  & =\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(\operatorname{ln}⁡2)} \\  & =\frac{1}{8}.\end{aligned}\end{aligned}
$$"} {:db/id 17592186317284 :question/id #uuid "92ca3690-4c8c-55b7-be9d-9370a5311a98" :question/math-academy-id "q-342583"} {:db/id 17592186317285 :question/id #uuid "e562f9e3-80f6-5a8e-be6e-6fda00e93b68" :question/math-academy-id "q-342584"} {:db/id 17592186317286 :question/id #uuid "eddd138b-b8bc-570d-b13d-ca41c49e9e70" :question/math-academy-id "q-342585"} {:db/id 17592186365453 :question/id #uuid "059a7498-ddb3-55e8-bcb5-59a377de1ec3" :question/math-academy-id "q-2525"} {:db/id 17592186409836 :question/id #uuid "9c5ee55a-541c-5782-b80c-3f5d6eae3256" :question/math-academy-id "q-15643" :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ecb3b6c-5f9d-519e-ac19-a3e22b342ea6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\frac{1}{6}" :db/id 17592186409823} {:answer/id #uuid "614852f7-d26e-56cc-93b6-f07f9c417f8d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\frac{1}{3}" :db/id 17592186409827} {:answer/id #uuid "8f042161-1e15-525d-8dc1-31568976162b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\frac{1}{6}" :db/id 17592186409828} {:answer/id #uuid "bc7bca2f-0e02-5b17-90a9-f17776a41ae8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\frac{1}{2}" :db/id 17592186409830} {:answer/id #uuid "ed1bca86-26ee-5bcf-a659-0ec759203aee" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id 17592186409831}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id 17592186409823} :answer-field/id #uuid "65142116-0d83-53ff-8a22-53c3c70d6112" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493} :db/id 17592186409833}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485} :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=12-6x$ and the point $P(3,-6)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ determine the slope of $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ at the point $(-6,3).$" :question/worked-solution "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(3,-6).$

