$$
{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(0)=\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))}
$$

First, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(0).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(0)=x.$ This gives

$$
\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(0) & =x \\ f(x) & =0 \\ (x+2)\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+1) & =0 \\ x & =-2,\,0.\end{aligned}
$$

We discard $x=-2$ since the logarithm is not defined for negative numbers. Therefore, ${f}^{-1}(0)=0.$

Now, we calculate ${f}^{′}(0),$ as follows:

$$
\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x+2)\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+1) \\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+1)+\frac{2(x+2)}{2x+1} \\ {f}^{′}(0) & =\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\cdot 0+1)+\frac{2(0+2)}{2\cdot 0+1} \\ {f}^{′}(0) & =0+4 \\ {f}^{′}(0) & =4\end{aligned}
$$

Finally, we get

$$
\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(0) & =\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\  & =\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(0)} \\  & =\frac{1}{4}.\end{aligned}
$$"} {:db/id 17592186345182 :question/id #uuid "f7615d87-ff46-5839-a075-3229075fb3df" :question/math-academy-id "q-76115"} {:db/id 17592186345184 :question/id #uuid "336c725c-310d-59e4-a4d6-4266c4a334d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-76128"} {:db/id 17592186366274 :question/id #uuid "67a843ac-f40c-59da-8377-455b0890a108" :question/math-academy-id "q-76108"} {:db/id 17592186505782 :question/id #uuid "70bc61eb-58b7-5349-9bef-f900f312799d" :question/math-academy-id "q-342615" :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "521f1d1c-c4a9-54ce-be0a-26a5043158cf" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id 17592186505268}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id 17592186505268} :answer-field/id #uuid "3dc0e4ad-a39d-502a-8eb5-af62880a7035" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045492} :db/id 17592186505640}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487} :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\sqrt{x}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2).$

