gonally Projects a Vector to the Subspace Spanned by an Eigenvector"} {:db/id 17592186161806 :question/id #uuid "d185ef42-c177-5217-99b7-b42d800f3385" :question/math-academy-id "e-8292"} {:db/id 17592186049489} {:db/id 17592186162646 :question/id #uuid "11bdef24-ff68-513b-bfa6-5f2fb8c78b02" :question/math-academy-id "q-94768" :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186162014 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84207049-12f7-514c-bfa2-51bce8631b41" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Projection Matrix That Orthogonally Projects a Vector to the Subspace Spanned by an Eigenvector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1556dc1b-0c8b-54e0-a340-18371f592ed3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\frac{1}{\sqrt{5}}\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\ -2 & 1\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186407520} {:answer/id #uuid "286d06a8-1050-59ed-a665-558d840c2aae" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\sqrt{5}\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\ -2 & 1\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186407525} {:answer/id #uuid "d6effcc8-e643-5b20-9090-b3855faacab9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\frac{1}{5}\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\ -1 & 1\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186407541} {:answer/id #uuid "e9f4f75f-447a-5e0c-a1d6-feae985a30a3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\ -1 & 1\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186407543} {:answer/id #uuid "f93a6cf9-78b6-546d-ba88-361fc48a056a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\frac{1}{5}\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\ -2 & 1\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186407544}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f93a6cf9-78b6-546d-ba88-361fc48a056a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\frac{1}{5}\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\ -2 & 1\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186407544} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebade115-5b0c-52d7-94cc-8ec9e5babad9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186407549}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection matrix that maps $x\in {R}^{2}$ onto the subspace spanned by an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\lambda =-4$ of the matrix $A=\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\ 4 & 4\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by

$$
A={\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},
$$

where ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.

Moreover, ${u}_{i}\cdot {u}_{i}^{T}$ is the projection matrix corresponding to ${u}_{i}.$

First, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\lambda =-4.$ Computing $A-(-4)I,$ we get

$$
\begin{aligned}A+4I & =\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\ 4 & 4\end{bmatrix}+4\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\ 0 & 1\end{bmatrix}=\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\ 4 & 8\end{bmatrix}.\end{aligned}
$$

So, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:

$$
\begin{aligned}M & =\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 0 \\ 4 & 8 & 0\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\  & ∼\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & 0\end{bmatrix}\end{aligned}
$$

The reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is

$$
\begin{bmatrix}-2{x}_{2} \\ {x}_{2}\end{bmatrix},\,{x}_{2}\in R.
$$

Therefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector 

$$
v=\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\ 1\end{bmatrix},
$$

where $‖v‖=\sqrt{5}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we obtain the normalized eigenvector: 

$$
u=\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{5}}\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\ 1\end{bmatrix}
$$

Finally, the required orthogonal projection matrix is given by

$$
\begin{aligned}u\cdot {u}^{T} & =\frac{1}{\sqrt{5}}\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\ 1\end{bmatrix}\cdot \frac{1}{\sqrt{5}}\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1\end{bmatrix} \\  & =\frac{1}{5}\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\ 1\end{bmatrix}\cdot \begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1\end{bmatrix} \\  & =\frac{1}{5}\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\ -2 & 1\end{bmatrix}.\end{aligned}
$$"} {:db/id 17592186162662 :question/id #uuid "28c27a06-cd3b-5e3d-a19e-4b46b9bccd18" :question/math-academy-id "q-95714" :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07faab27-15f7-5312-8b38-59ad02f72521" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\frac{1}{\sqrt{26}}\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 5 \\ 5 & -25\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186494338} {:answer/id #uuid "2f44ec68-5673-5d17-a202-807989a0d5ce" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\frac{1}{26}\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 5 \\ 5 & -25\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186494410} {:answer/id #uuid "6c89eafa-421f-5962-9af4-946ba03a1964" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\sqrt{26}\begin{bmatrix}25 & -5 \\ -5 & 1\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186494540} {:answer/id #uuid "8b205150-ffac-59f3-b8f8-30947cc22faa" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\frac{1}{\sqrt{26}}\begin{bmatrix}25 & -5 \\ -5 & 1\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186494635} {:answer/id #uuid "9d276ed0-9aab-5f35-92de-cd40b3be996e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\frac{1}{26}\begin{bmatrix}25 & -5 \\ -5 & 1\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186494678}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id 17592186494678} :answer-field/id #uuid "27a49c53-38b7-5dbb-b5f4-f47add37c5d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493} :db/id 17592186161264}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487} :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection matrix that maps $x\in {R}^{2}$ onto the subspace spanned by an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\lambda =-10$ of the matrix $A=\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 5 \\ 5 & 15\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by

$$
A={\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},
$$

where ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.

Moreover, ${u}_{i}\cdot {u}_{i}^{T}$ is the projection matrix corresponding to ${u}_{i}.$

First, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\lambda =-10.$ Computing $A-(-10)I,$ we get

$$
\begin{aligned}A+10I & =\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 5 \\ 5 & 15\end{bmatrix}+10\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\ 0 & 1\end{bmatrix}=\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\ 5 & 25\end{bmatrix}.\end{aligned}
$$

So, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:

$$
\begin{aligned}M & =\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & 0 \\ 5 & 25 & 0\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\  & ∼\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & 0\end{bmatrix}\end{aligned}
$$

The reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-5{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is

$$
\begin{bmatrix}-5{x}_{2} \\ {x}_{2}\end{bmatrix},\,{x}_{2}\in R.
$$

Therefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector 

$$
v=\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\ 1\end{bmatrix},
$$

where $‖v‖=\sqrt{26}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we obtain the normalized eigenvector: 

$$
u=\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{26}}\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\ 1\end{bmatrix}
$$

Finally, the required orthogonal projection matrix is given by

$$
\begin{aligned}u\cdot {u}^{T} & =\frac{1}{\sqrt{26}}\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\ 1\end{bmatrix}\cdot \frac{1}{\sqrt{26}}\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 1\end{bmatrix} \\  & =\frac{1}{26}\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\ 1\end{bmatrix}\cdot \begin{bmatrix}-5 & 1\end{bmatrix} \\  & =\frac{1}{26}\begin{bmatrix}25 & -5 \\ -5 & 1\end{bmatrix}.\end{aligned}
