3} :activity/first-step {:db/id 17592186407555} :activity/id #uuid "cb802846-7e96-5e29-a7fd-43a85cc938d8" :activity/scope {:db/id 17592186049849 :topic/id #uuid "1887f465-a1b8-5370-82f6-80e02b9d153a" :topic/math-academy-id 3120} :activity/steps [{:db/id 17592186407554 :step/content {:db/id 17592186407550 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "237e9827-741e-554d-b4bd-a580c12832ad" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\begin{bmatrix}5 & 3 \\ 3 & 5\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186407524} {:answer/id #uuid "637ff16c-2a36-5623-9258-1f3130b80075" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\ 3 & 2\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186407529} {:answer/id #uuid "6e8f4850-6613-5ef7-a355-f12825aba902" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\begin{bmatrix}10 & 6 \\ 6 & 10\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186407532} {:answer/id #uuid "bea7dec3-53c9-52fb-9b61-30210ef0b89a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\ 1 & 2\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186407538} {:answer/id #uuid "d23ed5d0-4d06-561d-902d-1213f1013134" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -5 \\ -5 & -3\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186407539}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id 17592186407524} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b7eee37-4841-5d7e-83f1-ad47a007de82" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493} :db/id 17592186407545}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "69f0b9bd-9336-5c1c-91e3-bf72b33b1333" :question/math-academy-id "q-140847" :question/problem "Suppose that

$\,$ ${v}_{1}=\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\ 1\end{bmatrix}\,$ and $\,{v}_{2}=\begin{bmatrix}1 \\ 1\end{bmatrix}$

are eigenvectors of a $2\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\lambda }_{2}=8,$ respectively. Find the matrix $A.$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by

$$
A={\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},
$$

where ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.

First, let's normalize our eigenvectors:

$$
\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\ 1\end{bmatrix} \\ {u}_{2} & =\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\begin{bmatrix}1 \\ 1\end{bmatrix}\end{aligned}
$$

Therefore, we have

$$
\begin{aligned}A & ={\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}{u}_{1}^{T}+{\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T} \\  & =2\cdot \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\ 1\end{bmatrix}\cdot \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1\end{bmatrix}+8\cdot \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\begin{bmatrix}1 \\ 1\end{bmatrix}\cdot \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1\end{bmatrix} \\  & =2\cdot \frac{1}{2}\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\ 1\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1\end{bmatrix}+8\cdot \frac{1}{2}\begin{bmatrix}1 \\ 1\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1\end{bmatrix} \\  & =\frac{1}{2}(2\cdot \begin{bmatrix}-1 \\ 1\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1\end{bmatrix}+8\cdot \begin{bmatrix}1 \\ 1\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1\end{bmatrix}) \\  & =\frac{1}{2}(2\cdot \begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\ -1 & 1\end{bmatrix}+8\cdot \begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\ 1 & 1\end{bmatrix}) \\  & =\frac{1}{2}\begin{bmatrix}10 & 6 \\ 6 & 10\end{bmatrix} \\  & =\begin{bmatrix}5 & 3 \\ 3 & 5\end{bmatrix}.\end{aligned}
$$"} :step/id #uuid "07386288-c5f3-55df-b73a-99cec25cb7d6"} {:db/id 17592186407555 :step/content {:db/id 17592186162646 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1556dc1b-0c8b-54e0-a340-18371f592ed3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\frac{1}{\sqrt{5}}\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\ -2 & 1\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186407520} {:answer/id #uuid "286d06a8-1050-59ed-a665-558d840c2aae" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\sqrt{5}\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\ -2 & 1\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186407525} {:answer/id #uuid "d6effcc8-e643-5b20-9090-b3855faacab9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\frac{1}{5}\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\ -1 & 1\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186407541} {:answer/id #uuid "e9f4f75f-447a-5e0c-a1d6-feae985a30a3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\ -1 & 1\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186407543} {:answer/id #uuid "f93a6cf9-78b6-546d-ba88-361fc48a056a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\frac{1}{5}\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\ -2 & 1\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186407544}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id 17592186407544} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebade115-5b0c-52d7-94cc-8ec9e5babad9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493} :db/id 17592186407549}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "11bdef24-ff68-513b-bfa6-5f2fb8c78b02" :question/math-academy-id "q-94768" :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection matrix that maps $x\in {R}^{2}$ onto the subspace spanned by an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\lambda =-4$ of the matrix $A=\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\ 4 & 4\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by

$$
A={\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},
$$

where ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.

Moreover, ${u}_{i}\cdot {u}_{i}^{T}$ is the projection matrix corresponding to ${u}_{i}.$

First, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\lambda =-4.$ Computing $A-(-4)I,$ we get

$$
\begin{aligned}A+4I & =\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\ 4 & 4\end{bmatrix}+4\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\ 0 & 1\end{bmatrix}=\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\ 4 & 8\end{bmatrix}.\end{aligned}
$$

So, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:

$$
\begin{aligned}M & =\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 0 \\ 4 & 8 & 0\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\  & ∼\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & 0\end{bmatrix}\end{aligned}
$$

The reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is

$$
\begin{bmatrix}-2{x}_{2} \\ {x}_{2}\end{bmatrix},\,{x}_{2}\in R.
$$

Therefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector 

$$
v=\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\ 1\end{bmatrix},
$$

where $‖v‖=\sqrt{5}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we obtain the normalized eigenvector: 

$$
u=\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{5}}\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\ 1\end{bmatrix}
$$

Finally, the required orthogonal projection matrix is given by

$$
\begin{aligned}u\cdot {u}^{T} & =\frac{1}{\sqrt{5}}\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\ 1\end{bmatrix}\cdot \frac{1}{\sqrt{5}}\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1\end{bmatrix} \\  & =\frac{1}{5}\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\ 1\end{bmatrix}\cdot \begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1\end{bmatrix} \\  & =\frac{1}{5}\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\ -2 & 1\end{bmatrix}.\end{aligned}
$$"} :step/id #uuid "2d35b336-2654-53f7-8116-4689735d233e" :step/next {:db/id 17592186407557}} {:db/id 17592186407556 :step/content {:db/id 17592186407551 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16877fa0-d3a3-5da8-ba77-c7e71244a7c0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\begin{bmatrix}0 & -6 \\ -6 & 4\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186407521} {:answer/id #uuid "6d264661-257d-5c8a-8684-122b5c0a6807" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 6 \\ 6 & 0\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186407531} {:answer/id #uuid "9555f6b0-dd96-561e-b956-a28b10613e7e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\begin{bmatrix}-8 & 6 \\ 6 & -3\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186407535} {:answer/id #uuid "ad0b779d-af50-58f8-97aa-dd75027108dd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\begin{bmatrix}5 & -6 \\ -6 & -4\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186407536} {:answer/id #uuid "d6231cbd-de79-555a-b056-b8008595ed25" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 6 \\ 6 & -3\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186407540}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id 17592186407531} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1092e23-b9a3-5556-b0d1-0745f95be4b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493} :db/id 17592186407548}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7e3f233e-913d-50cf-8912-1c6832afca12" :question/math-academy-id "q-95725" :question/problem "Suppose that

$\,$ ${v}_{1}=\begin{bmatrix}2 \\ 3\end{bmatrix}\,$ and $\,{v}_{2}=\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\ 2\end{bmatrix}$

are eigenvectors of a $2\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\lambda }_{2}=-9,$ respectively. Find the matrix $A.$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by

$$
A={\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},
$$

where ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.

First, let's normalize our eigenvectors:

$$
\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{13}}\begin{bmatrix}2 \\ 3\end{bmatrix} \\ {u}_{2} & =\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{13}}\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\ 2\end{bmatrix}\end{aligned}
$$

Therefore, we have

$$
\begin{aligned}A & ={\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}{u}_{1}^{T}+{\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T} \\  & =4\cdot \frac{1}{\sqrt{13}}\begin{bmatrix}2 \\ 3\end{bmatrix}\cdot \frac{1}{\sqrt{13}}\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3\end{bmatrix}+(-9)\cdot \frac{1}{\sqrt{13}}\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\ 2\end{bmatrix}\cdot \frac{1}{\sqrt{13}}\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2\end{bmatrix} \\  & =4\cdot \frac{1}{13}\begin{bmatrix}2 \\ 3\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3\end{bmatrix}+(-9)\cdot \frac{1}{13}\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\ 2\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2\end{bmatrix} \\  & =\frac{1}{13}(4\cdot \begin{bmatrix}2 \\ 3\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3\end{bmatrix}+(-9)\cdot \begin{bmatrix}-3 \\ 2\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2\end{bmatrix}) \\  & =\frac{1}{13}(4\cdot \begin{bmatrix}4 & 6 \\ 6 & 9\end{bmatrix}+(-9)\cdot \begin{bmatrix}9 & -6 \\ -6 & 4\end{bmatrix}) \\  & =\frac{1}{13}\begin{bmatrix}-65 & 78 \\ 78 & 0\end{bmatrix} \\  & =\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 6 \\ 6 & 0\end{bmatrix}.\end{aligned}
$$"} :step/id #uuid "5fc71f89-cc9a-549d-9f1f-b3a2081e4a63" :step/next {:db/id 17592186407554}} {:db/id 17592186407557 :step/content {:db/id 17592186407552 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d327dec-d42a-581c-8707-926d0b6893a9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\frac{1}{17}\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 \\ -4 & 16\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186407522} {:answer/id #uuid "3c3ec327-8643-5dfe-8735-8c0774db03ee" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\frac{1}{\sqrt{17}}\begin{bmatrix}16 & 4 \\ 4 & 1\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186407526} {:answer/id #uuid "799755e8-7ecf-5b56-8460-da2805b858a9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\sqrt{17}\begin{bmatrix}16 & 4 \\ 4 & 1\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186407533} {:answer/id #uuid "95054c16-a500-55cc-9c6d-5dc257b93966" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\frac{1}{\sqrt{17}}\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 \\ -4 & 16\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186407534} {:answer/id #uuid "e4b2cd88-0c1c-5067-96b8-f34a70eda38d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\frac{1}{17}\begin{bmatrix}16 & 4 \\ 4 & 1\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186407542}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id 17592186407542} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb85388f-97d4-5b7a-9384-bb45d3a9d42b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493} :db/id 17592186407547}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9d231abd-db3d-5d11-9253-820e7eb51742" :question/math-academy-id "q-95712" :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection matrix that maps $x\in {R}^{2}$ onto the subspace spanned by an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\lambda =2$ of the matrix $A=\begin{bmatrix}3 & -4 \\ -4 & 18\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by

$$
