The reduced singular value decomposition of $A$ is then given by

$$
A = U_r \Sigma_r V_r^T.
$$

So, in our example, we have

![](images/c1/c1eed35f9b7aa3214e9783f8f80952e57adca6c4d4b81efaeb49255b5973f3af.svg)

In general, a singular value decomposition 

$$
A=U \Sigma V^T
$$



of an $m \times n$ matrix $A$ is not unique. However, we can always simplify it to the corresponding reduced singular value decomposition 

$$
A=U_r \Sigma_r V_r^T
$$



with the following properties:

- $\Sigma_r$ is a square diagonal matrix of size $r\times r,$ where $r\leq \min\{m,n\}$ is the rank of $A.$ The main diagonal of $\Sigma_r$ contains the non-zero singular values $\sigma_1, \sigma_2, \ldots, \sigma_r$ of $A$ in descending order.

- $U_r$ is a $m\times r$ matrix obtained by keeping only the first $r$ columns of $U.$

- $V_r$ is a $n \times r$ matrix obtained by keeping only the first $r$ columns of $V.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "924b4229-0b49-5d81-9255-b9be0ec07ab6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6075 :tutorial/title "Reduced Singular Value Decomposition"} {:db/id 17592186163035 :step/content {:db/id 17592186162091 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186161904 :question/id #uuid "66c34c76-6243-50b8-832f-027abb6ab736" :question/math-academy-id "e-9830"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff858037-da99-53e9-acdc-e4963e7d765e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186162348} {:db/id 17592186162359} {:db/id 17592186371810} {:db/id 17592186383389} {:db/id 17592186383396} {:db/id 17592186388552} {:db/id 17592186388559} {:db/id 17592186388566} {:db/id 17592186420790} {:db/id 17592186420791} {:db/id 17592186420792}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Pseudoinverse of a Matrix Given Its Reduced Singular Value Decomposition"} :step/id #uuid "03964208-b7f5-5c5d-ab41-385b66f7efbd" :step/math-academy-id 36970 :step/next {:db/id 17592186163039}} {:db/id 17592186163039 :step/content {:db/id 17592186163309 :tutorial/content "Recall that the least-squares solution of the system 

$$
