a_0^{(h)}=a_0+2C,
\qquad
a_n^{(h)}=a_n,
\qquad
b_n^{(h)}=b_n.
$$

In the next slides, we will apply this rule to a concrete example." :tutorial/id #uuid "97a65ef1-7e05-5e99-9172-ee34abb62f01" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14645 :tutorial/title "Fourier Series Properties: Vertical Shifting"} {:db/id 17592186230099 :step/content {:db/id 17592186230239 :tutorial/content "Another simple transformation is *reflecting a function across the $y$-axis*.

Again, assume $f$ has the Fourier series

$$
f(x)\sim \dfrac{a_0}{2}+\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\big(a_n\cos(nx)+b_n\sin(nx)\big).
$$

If $h(x)=f(-x),$ then cosine terms stay the same while sine terms change sign (because $\cos(nx)$ is even and $\sin(nx)$ is odd):

$$
h(x)\sim \dfrac{a_0}{2}+\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\big(a_n\cos(nx)-b_n\sin(nx)\big).
$$

So, the coefficients of the transformed series are

$$
a_0^{(h)}=a_0,
\qquad
a_n^{(h)}=a_n,
\qquad
b_n^{(h)}=-b_n.
$$

Let's see how to apply this rule in practice on the next slides." :tutorial/id #uuid "71af7376-dcfe-571f-96a9-28dbce65cb76" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14855 :tutorial/title "Fourier Series Properties: Horizontal Reflection"} :step/id #uuid "06e400a8-93bf-593c-8c80-0ecb3f2ca60b" :step/math-academy-id 606272 :step/next {:db/id 17592186230071}} {:db/id 17592186230071 :step/content {:db/id 17592186229174 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186228941 :question/id #uuid "848cc0af-b940-5ead-81ac-49cc6d2f4989" :question/math-academy-id "e-21805"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f30ca427-3074-56c3-828a-1327d7b172b0" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id 17592186050647} {:db/id 17592186050962}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186229719} {:db/id 17592186229720} {:db/id 17592186315018} {:db/id 17592186315019} {:db/id 17592186315020} {:db/id 17592186315021} {:db/id 17592186315022} {:db/id 17592186417679} {:db/id 17592186417680} {:db/id 17592186417681}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Effects of Vertical Shifting and Horizontal Reflection on Coefficients"} :step/id #uuid "adb6b87d-19f4-5dca-a59c-1ae6ecde0e28" :step/math-academy-id 600914 :step/next {:db/id 17592186230072}} {:db/id 17592186230072 :step/content {:db/id 17592186230218 :tutorial/content "When a $2\pi$-periodic function has symmetry, many of its Fourier coefficients must be zero. This can simplify Fourier series computations dramatically.

Assume $f(x)$ is $2\pi$-periodic and piecewise continuous on $[-\pi,\pi),$ with Fourier series

$$
f(x)\sim \dfrac{a_0}{2}+\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\big(a_n\cos(nx)+b_n\sin(nx)\big).
$$

We will use the property that the integral of an odd function over a symmetric interval like $[-\pi, \pi]$ is always zero.

- If $f$ is an *even* function, meaning $f(-x)=f(x),$ then all sine coefficients $b_n$ must be zero.
This is because each $b_n$ is calculated from an integral of an *odd* function:

$$
b_n = \dfrac{1}{\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi} \underbrace{f(x)}_{\text{even}} \, \underbrace{\sin(nx)}_{\text{odd}} \, \text{d}x = \dfrac{1}{\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi} (\text{odd function})\,\text{d}x = 0.
$$

The Fourier series of an even function has *cosine terms only*:

$$
f(x)\sim \dfrac{a_0}{2}+\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}a_n\cos(nx)
$$

- If $f$ is an *odd* function, meaning $f(-x)=-f(x),$ then the constant and all cosine coefficients (both $a_0$ and $a_n$) must be zero.
This is because these coefficients are calculated from integrals of *odd* functions:

$$
a_n = \dfrac{1}{\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi} \underbrace{f(x)}_{\text{odd}} \, \underbrace{\cos(nx)}_{\text{even}} \,\text{d}x = \dfrac{1}{\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi} (\text{odd function})\,\text{d}x = 0 \quad (\text{for } n \ge 0).
$$

The Fourier series of an odd function has *sine terms only*:

$$
f(x)\sim \sum_{n=1}^{\infty}b_n\sin(nx)
$$

**Note:** We may use either $[-\pi,\pi)$ or $[-\pi,\pi]$ as the base interval. Including or excluding the endpoints does not affect the conclusions.

Let's see how this works on concrete examples below." :tutorial/id #uuid "dd9a59ae-0e83-5c4d-9b72-d4db4e1e202a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14644 :tutorial/title "Fourier Series of Odd and Even Functions"} :step/id #uuid "814960c8-4765-557a-b06d-bfea6027ccc2" :step/math-academy-id 600917 :step/next {:db/id 17592186230073}} {:db/id 17592186230218 :tutorial/content "When a $2\pi$-periodic function has symmetry, many of its Fourier coefficients must be zero. This can simplify Fourier series computations dramatically.

