Because the function is *concave up* over the interval ![](images/85/8559262277e3f52fd1eb8139fad407ed50f80873ec56c7349969a9a3ee6789e4.svg) the trapezoids reach above the function, and therefore our approximation is an *overestimate* of the actual area." :tutorial/id #uuid "e65800fc-8bfa-5eef-910a-646db63059f3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 745 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a39b94fd-84fb-50cb-a6e2-54562afa24b2" :step/math-academy-id 1433 :step/next {:db/id 17592186093339}} {:db/id 17592186092986 :step/content {:db/id 17592186092179 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186092005 :question/id #uuid "d5b950ed-cb5e-5160-a0ca-c88c8bf8f2af" :question/math-academy-id "e-398"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "32793ffd-b4c6-58af-9f6f-69c756ed7f1d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id 17592186048147} {:db/id 17592186048363} {:db/id 17592186049386}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186092740} {:db/id 17592186092743} {:db/id 17592186319151} {:db/id 17592186323803} {:db/id 17592186323804} {:db/id 17592186354417} {:db/id 17592186354418}] :knowledge-point/title "Using the Trapezoidal Rule with Regular Step Size"} :step/id #uuid "a4629462-9622-5f78-a714-26307a2c193d" :step/math-academy-id 1434 :step/next {:db/id 17592186093144}} {:db/id 17592186092987 :step/content {:db/id 17592186093490 :tutorial/content "So far, the step size $\Delta x$ has been the same for each trapezoid. In these cases, we say that the step size is **regular**.

