However, in some situations, the step size can vary. In such cases, we say that the step size is **irregular**. But we can still use the trapezoidal rule to approximate the area under a curve. Let's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "cdc39254-77da-5bea-bcaf-2e7e1b965777" :tutorial/math-academy-id 748 :tutorial/title "Trapezoidal Rule Sum with Irregular Step Size"} {:db/id 17592186092988 :step/content {:db/id 17592186092180 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186092006 :question/id #uuid "902fb0e4-47fd-53b7-9755-b5cbe49fd9be" :question/math-academy-id "e-399"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bec73486-156f-5124-97b4-58f65ea8430d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186092741} {:db/id 17592186092859} {:db/id 17592186253254} {:db/id 17592186253255} {:db/id 17592186319154} {:db/id 17592186354425} {:db/id 17592186354431} {:db/id 17592186354432}] :knowledge-point/title "Using the Trapezoidal Rule With Irregular Step Size"} :step/id #uuid "502bd3d3-92d7-5b81-9e46-de4cbc0abae7" :step/math-academy-id 1439 :step/next {:db/id 17592186092989}} {:db/id 17592186092989 :step/content {:db/id 17592186092181 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186092007 :question/id #uuid "609e4a44-e2cc-5980-806e-a1ebd27f56ea" :question/math-academy-id "e-400"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "903cd6d7-18f2-5b53-a8e9-9aaef5589549" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186092742} {:db/id 17592186092744} {:db/id 17592186253277} {:db/id 17592186297430} {:db/id 17592186319160} {:db/id 17592186332682} {:db/id 17592186332846} {:db/id 17592186332847} {:db/id 17592186399912}] :knowledge-point/title "Using the Trapezoidal Rule: Word Problem"} :step/id #uuid "77f0ef8a-4a60-5548-98f5-3232466916a7" :step/math-academy-id 1442} {:db/id 17592186093381 :tutorial/content "In the previous problem, we had the following expression for the trapezoidal rule sum:

$$
\mathcal{A} \approx \dfrac{ {\color{red}f(1)}+{\color{red}f(2)} }{2}\cdot {\color{blue}\Delta x} + \dfrac{ {\color{red}f(2)}+ {\color{red}f(3)} }{2}\cdot {\color{blue}\Delta x} + \dfrac{ {\color{red}f(3)}+ {\color{red}f(4)} }{2}\cdot {\color{blue}\Delta x}
$$

Notice that we can simplify the above by factoring out $\dfrac{1}{2}$ and $\Delta x,$ and also collecting like terms:

$$
\mathcal{A} \approx \dfrac{1}{2} [ \overbrace{{\color{red}f(1)} + 2\left( {\color{red}f(2)}+{\color{red}f(3)}\right) +{\color{red}f(4)}}^{\color{red}\large\text{bases}} ] \underbrace{\color{blue}\Delta x}_{\color{blue}\large\text{height}}
$$

In general, we can compute the trapezoidal rule sum for a function $y=f(x)$ over an interval $[a,b]$ using the **general formula** as follows:

$$
\mathcal{A} \approx \dfrac{1}{2}\cdot [\overbrace{{\color{red}f(a)} + 2({\color{red}f(x_1)}+{\color{red}f(x_2)}+ \ldots +{\color{red}f(x_{n-1})}) +{\color{red}f(b)} }^{\color{red}\large\text{bases}} ] \cdot\!\! \underbrace{\color{blue}\Delta x}_{\color{blue}\large\text{height}},
$$

Here, $n$ is the number of trapezoids, and $\Delta x$ is the step size given by

$$
\Delta x = \dfrac{b-a}{n}.
$$

**Note:** If we look carefully at the formula, we realize that the trapezoidal rule is an average of the left and right Riemann sums!" :tutorial/id #uuid "4c91f011-2a77-5e6e-b606-b2f1ba9109a5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2025 :tutorial/title "The General Formula"} {:activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id 17592186093335} :activity/id #uuid "21c10e32-8c2e-5d6b-a5cb-19f07aa83fdf" :activity/scope {:db/id 17592186049236 :topic/id #uuid "e5b028a3-9410-51df-925f-0bb38d65ee4b" :topic/math-academy-id 993} :activity/steps [{:db/id 17592186093089 :step/content {:db/id 17592186092242 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186092084 :question/id #uuid "6e4ab84e-f722-557e-9740-25b3a0b3d660" :question/math-academy-id "e-6104"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b175cb1-6615-540f-9145-224a838e37d0" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186092715} {:db/id 17592186092730} {:db/id 17592186317439} {:db/id 17592186329819} {:db/id 17592186339054} {:db/id 17592186339055} {:db/id 17592186508057} {:db/id 17592186508070} {:db/id 17592186508097}] :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative Given a Graph Using a Backward Difference Approximation"} :step/id #uuid "332d8f20-0643-51b2-8b43-875b7569adc3" :step/math-academy-id 22597 :step/next {:db/id 17592186093337}} {:db/id 17592186093090 :step/content {:db/id 17592186092243 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186092085 :question/id #uuid "fb3d12e8-e413-5300-8d3f-991ddfd2a893" :question/math-academy-id "e-6105"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d864064-2833-530a-b421-a57f9ec5fce5" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186092729} {:db/id 17592186092731} {:db/id 17592186317444} {:db/id 17592186317445} {:db/id 17592186317446} {:db/id 17592186329809} {:db/id 17592186354476} {:db/id 17592186508058} {:db/id 17592186508074}] :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative Given a Graph Using a Backward Difference Approximation: Word Problem"} :step/id #uuid "0358e8c8-7e96-5bb6-9fc7-eef0c1c3cde8" :step/math-academy-id 22598 :step/next {:db/id 17592186093338}} {:db/id 17592186093335 :step/content {:db/id 17592186093444 :tutorial/content "To approximate the derivative of a function, we can use the finite difference formula

$$
f'(x)\approx \frac{f(x) - f(x-h) }{h}
$$

for small values of the step size $h,$ with $h>0.$

This expression is known as the **backward difference approximation** because if $h>0$ then $x-h$ is a step behind $x$ (i.e., to the left). 

