As usual, the smaller the step size $h$ is, the better the approximation to $f'(x)$ we obtain." :tutorial/id #uuid "6aff8fa6-f787-5984-944a-5648207e6830" :tutorial/math-academy-id 368 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "5a2b7913-8af1-56e3-ae9c-6dea1ca15630" :step/math-academy-id 686 :step/next {:db/id 17592186093336}} {:db/id 17592186093336 :step/content {:db/id 17592186092390 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186091959 :question/id #uuid "7a6fbfc8-cd6d-57fe-bcab-ab68339683bd" :question/math-academy-id "e-155"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "494ef4f7-873c-5d87-85e5-89094c6cc376" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id 17592186049061} {:db/id 17592186049362}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186092920} {:db/id 17592186092921} {:db/id 17592186317435} {:db/id 17592186317436} {:db/id 17592186317437} {:db/id 17592186339050} {:db/id 17592186339053} {:db/id 17592186508041} {:db/id 17592186508094} {:db/id 17592186508101}] :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative Given a Table Using a Backward Difference Approximation"} :step/id #uuid "f1579afd-f8b8-522d-815c-72dc7b18e4b7" :step/math-academy-id 687 :step/next {:db/id 17592186093089}} {:db/id 17592186093337 :step/content {:db/id 17592186092391 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186091961 :question/id #uuid "cdf8854d-14ea-5564-81cc-4e5098f1acc3" :question/math-academy-id "e-156"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6068e54-fa91-5078-86f8-d34f1f1a3ca4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id 17592186048835}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186092727} {:db/id 17592186092728} {:db/id 17592186317440} {:db/id 17592186317442} {:db/id 17592186329817} {:db/id 17592186332871} {:db/id 17592186339056} {:db/id 17592186508052} {:db/id 17592186508064} {:db/id 17592186508068} {:db/id 17592186508082}] :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative Given a Table Using a Backward Difference Approximation: Word Problem"} :step/id #uuid "84a50349-fb71-5a17-b1e7-276d47f34414" :step/math-academy-id 691 :step/next {:db/id 17592186093090}} {:db/id 17592186093338 :step/content {:db/id 17592186093445 :tutorial/content "Where does the formula for the backward difference come from? Remember that from the definition of derivative, we have the forward difference formula

$$
f'(x)\approx \frac{f(x+h) - f(x) }{h}
$$

for small values of  $h.$ 

Now, replacing $h$ with $-h$ in the formula above gives

$$
\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ≈\frac{f(x-h)-f(x)}{-h} \\  & =\frac{f(x)-f(x-h)}{h}.\end{aligned}
$$

