$$

The step of replacing $h$ with $-h$ is equivalent to stepping a distance of $h$ to the left rather than to the right." :tutorial/id #uuid "1cc2d207-71d4-53a4-8c67-5af0c5f4d173" :tutorial/math-academy-id 371 :tutorial/title "Derivation of the Formula"} {:activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id 17592186093330} :activity/id #uuid "3cb60590-ce3c-5ae5-bb4c-9fa261e5f9f2" :activity/scope {:db/id 17592186049934 :topic/id #uuid "1fbdb06d-f1f4-55f3-bf00-af01b659732d" :topic/math-academy-id 992} :activity/steps [{:db/id 17592186093330 :step/content {:db/id 17592186093443 :tutorial/content "For a function $f(x),$ its derivative at a point $x$ is defined by the limit

$$
f'(x) = \lim\limits_{h\to 0} \frac{f(x+h) - f(x) }{h}.
$$

But often, computing this limit exactly is impossible or impractical. One example might be when we don't have an explicit expression for $f(x).$ In such cases, instead of finding an exact value for $f'(x)$, we can *approximate* the derivative using the formula



$$
f'(x)\approx \frac{f(x+h) - f(x) }{h}
$$

for small values of the "step size" $h,$ with $h>0.$

This expression is called the **forward difference approximation** because if $h >0$ then $x+h$ is a step forward (i.e., to the right) from $x.$

