333 :step/content {:db/id 17592186092388 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186091957 :question/id #uuid "580533af-c13d-5e1c-8282-f0172709c7e1" :question/math-academy-id "e-153"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e651cb4-ae24-5ac7-9c01-d90de562b155" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id 17592186048835}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186092716} {:db/id 17592186092717} {:db/id 17592186317198} {:db/id 17592186317199} {:db/id 17592186329811} {:db/id 17592186339015} {:db/id 17592186505746} {:db/id 17592186505779} {:db/id 17592186505842}] :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative From a Table Using a Forward Difference Approximation: Word Problem"} :step/id #uuid "7e8c5132-4661-56c0-9cc7-9cd625cd092f" :step/math-academy-id 682 :step/next {:db/id 17592186093334}} {:db/id 17592186093334 :step/content {:db/id 17592186092389 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186091958 :question/id #uuid "73818e0b-6f95-59a6-b1a7-672bbea8de86" :question/math-academy-id "e-154"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "167f04f0-c08e-51d8-acfa-2c4660083e6a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186092718} {:db/id 17592186092721} {:db/id 17592186297362} {:db/id 17592186317204} {:db/id 17592186317205} {:db/id 17592186317206} {:db/id 17592186505821} {:db/id 17592186505833}] :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative From a Graph Using a Forward Difference Approximation: Word Problem"} :step/id #uuid "712fc50f-c422-5419-9b82-fa1b3e7a4a00" :step/math-academy-id 684}] :activity/title "Estimating Derivatives Using a Forward Difference Quotient" :activity/type {:db/id 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id 17592186092466} {:db/id 17592186093330 :step/content {:db/id 17592186093443 :tutorial/content "For a function $f(x),$ its derivative at a point $x$ is defined by the limit

$$
f'(x) = \lim\limits_{h\to 0} \frac{f(x+h) - f(x) }{h}.
$$

But often, computing this limit exactly is impossible or impractical. One example might be when we don't have an explicit expression for $f(x).$ In such cases, instead of finding an exact value for $f'(x)$, we can *approximate* the derivative using the formula



$$
f'(x)\approx \frac{f(x+h) - f(x) }{h}
$$

for small values of the "step size" $h,$ with $h>0.$

This expression is called the **forward difference approximation** because if $h >0$ then $x+h$ is a step forward (i.e., to the right) from $x.$

![](images/18/1860d33c3b34fa5c869f5a3c7a04ef4d02271ba30d60f973b892800ef7c9524e.png)

Generally, this and other similar methods are known as **finite-difference** approximations. The smaller the value of $h,$ the better the approximation." :tutorial/id #uuid "c3fb3f8f-2a89-5f4a-8f24-9cf556a18c41" :tutorial/math-academy-id 363 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "c0976a2b-1337-5627-8c4d-ef63079d733d" :step/math-academy-id 676 :step/next {:db/id 17592186093331}} {:db/id 17592186093443 :tutorial/content "For a function $f(x),$ its derivative at a point $x$ is defined by the limit

