$$
\displaystyle\sum_{i=1}^m\sum_{j=1}^n a_{ij}= \sum_{j=1}^n\sum_{i=1}^m a_{ij}
$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d25ceabe-e67a-59b3-9ed6-37be45644e5d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6584 :tutorial/title "The Product and Swap Rules"} {:activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id 17592186134627} :activity/id #uuid "79df4a45-c922-5703-9054-3312861d012f" :activity/scope {:db/id 17592186048849 :topic/id #uuid "c5e321c1-6c8d-5f19-b5bc-d257741addc3" :topic/math-academy-id 2095} :activity/steps [{:db/id 17592186134627 :step/content {:db/id 17592186135031 :tutorial/content "The vector space $\mathbb{R}^n,$ paired with the dot product, is called a **Euclidean space**. 

For a vector ![](images/83/838ca0a3284985fa58d5ac894740b15f9bbbfd931299834cc8e062e364bbf983.svg) in $\mathbb{R}^n,$  the **norm** (or **length**)  is given by 

$$
\Vert \mathbf{x} \Vert = \sqrt{\mathbf{x} \cdot \mathbf{x}} = \sqrt{x_1^2+x_2^2+\ldots+x_n^2}.
$$

For example, consider the vector ![](images/2a/2a4e0070bdec6033ab8b71cbb77dbd8569bea8672c0e17cedbf876a77ce56bb2.svg) The norm of this vector is calculated as

$$
