Note that the dot product of a vector with itself is always non-negative, $\mathbf{x}\cdot\mathbf{x}\geq 0,$ so it's always possible to take the square root $\sqrt{ \mathbf{x}\cdot\mathbf{x} }.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "cf55fe35-583a-57a7-9a58-529f60897c64" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3602 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "4d2c70c6-8130-51bd-bba0-328eb720e63e" :step/math-academy-id 20183 :step/next {:db/id 17592186134628}} {:db/id 17592186134628 :step/content {:db/id 17592186133759 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186133568 :question/id #uuid "0ab996ae-e3a7-5353-87c2-9b2a01f4f808" :question/math-academy-id "e-5538"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d893f937-d802-5d27-b91a-b78caee0313e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id 17592186049045}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186134203} {:db/id 17592186134206} {:db/id 17592186271979} {:db/id 17592186342155} {:db/id 17592186342156} {:db/id 17592186423588} {:db/id 17592186423594} {:db/id 17592186423595} {:db/id 17592186423599}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Norm of a Vector"} :step/id #uuid "41dc1ce1-3949-5bd4-a737-625779c43d60" :step/math-academy-id 20184 :step/next {:db/id 17592186134735}} {:db/id 17592186134629 :step/content {:db/id 17592186133760 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186133569 :question/id #uuid "f2ff1620-28e1-59ba-97ca-c43f3f300fdf" :question/math-academy-id "e-5539"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff66eef8-74b6-5553-af34-7da5a8cbbb02" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186134204} {:db/id 17592186134237} {:db/id 17592186271980} {:db/id 17592186271981} {:db/id 17592186326752} {:db/id 17592186326781} {:db/id 17592186342158} {:db/id 17592186423589} {:db/id 17592186423597}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distance Between Two Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "b2bd1009-695c-5967-93d6-75e93251bdf5" :step/math-academy-id 20185 :step/next {:db/id 17592186134737}} {:db/id 17592186134630 :step/content {:db/id 17592186135032 :tutorial/content "A **unit vector** is a vector whose length is equal to $1.$ If we divide a *non-zero* vector $\mathbf{x}$ by its norm $\Vert \mathbf{x} \Vert,$ we obtain a unit vector $\mathbf{v}=\dfrac{\mathbf{x}}{\Vert\mathbf{x}\Vert},$ since

$$
\Vert \mathbf{v} \Vert	=\left\Vert \dfrac{\mathbf{x}}{\Vert \mathbf{x} \Vert} \right\Vert
						= \dfrac{1}{\Vert \mathbf{x} \Vert}\cdot\Vert \mathbf{x} \Vert
						= 1.
$$

The process of dividing a vector $\mathbf{x}$ by its norm $\Vert \mathbf{x} \Vert$ is called **normalizing the vector $\mathbf{x}.$** We say that the unit vector $\mathbf{v}=\dfrac{\mathbf{x}}{\Vert\mathbf{x}\Vert}$ is *in the same direction* as $\mathbf{x}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "65cfbc34-e842-5e88-aaa8-f40b697f1b1f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3603 :tutorial/title "Normalizing a Vector"} :step/id #uuid "33e38aa0-acfb-5a0e-87b4-4d4fdfb38455" :step/math-academy-id 20186 :step/next {:db/id 17592186134631}} {:db/id 17592186134631 :step/content {:db/id 17592186133761 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186133570 :question/id #uuid "7b7adf29-1653-5de7-915c-80a09f05995a" :question/math-academy-id "e-5540"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae41d849-9def-540c-8985-8fbeec679991" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186134205} {:db/id 17592186134207} {:db/id 17592186326785} {:db/id 17592186342162} {:db/id 17592186393397} {:db/id 17592186403759} {:db/id 17592186423590} {:db/id 17592186423591} {:db/id 17592186423593} {:db/id 17592186423596} {:db/id 17592186423598}] :knowledge-point/title "Normalizing a Vector"} :step/id #uuid "cd0fcef8-cb65-59c0-81f7-f73c86d09b7e" :step/math-academy-id 20187} {:db/id 17592186134735 :step/content {:db/id 17592186135073 :tutorial/content "In a Euclidean space, the **distance** between the vectors $\mathbf{x}$ and $\mathbf{y}$ is the norm of the difference $\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{y},$ that is

$$
\text{d}(\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{y}) = \Vert \mathbf{x} - \mathbf{y} \Vert.
$$

To create a formula for the distance, we write the vectors in component form:
![](images/01/011499b685af9a4c78d195755dc34e7b0d6ea0292eb2e9feafb94312fc7ee4b3.svg) 

Now, we can express the norm in terms of the components:

$$
\begin{aligned}\text{d}(x,y) & =‖x-y‖ \\  & =\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{y}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{y}_{2})^{2}+⋯+({x}_{n}-{y}_{n})^{2}}\end{aligned}
$$

For example, we can use the formula to find the distance between the vectors ![](images/2e/2e5d7cbac29024ca7f343363598714ea17bb180036d2f50f094a25584be2e23e.svg) and ![](images/37/37364e71c36f25701aa2e3b0ad368d4567b5719aa0c884ff0406ee9686125808.svg) as follows:

$$
\begin{aligned}\text{d}(x,y) & =‖x-y‖ \\  & =\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{y}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{y}_{2})^{2}} \\  & =\sqrt{(1-(-5))^{2}+(4-1)^{2}} \\  & =\sqrt{{6}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\  & =\sqrt{45} \\  & =3\sqrt{5}\end{aligned}
$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "00353d62-b024-52a9-a1ad-744f7f9e1492" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3923 :tutorial/title "The Distance Between Two Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "c4535616-82fc-59ed-962a-9ca36d7214a8" :step/math-academy-id 23165 :step/next {:db/id 17592186134629}} {:db/id 17592186134736 :step/content {:db/id 17592186133824 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186133639 :question/id #uuid "86cf381d-8a69-5290-806b-09477c7ff92e" :question/math-academy-id "e-6190"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "837e0fa4-b8d3-59c1-87c7-44317adafaf9" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186134238} {:db/id 17592186134239} {:db/id 17592186271982} {:db/id 17592186342159} {:db/id 17592186342160} {:db/id 17592186342161} {:db/id 17592186392320} {:db/id 17592186423587} {:db/id 17592186423592} {:db/id 17592186423600}] :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Constant Out of the Norm"} :step/id #uuid "bba7150c-8928-5dd6-b8e9-d9d8983db01f" :step/math-academy-id 23169 :step/next {:db/id 17592186134630}} {:db/id 17592186134737 :step/content {:db/id 17592186135074 :tutorial/content "We can factor a constant out of the norm, provided that we take the absolute value of the constant:

$$
\Vert k \mathbf{x} \Vert= \vert k \vert \cdot \Vert \mathbf{x} \Vert
$$

For example, consider the vector ![](images/43/430769431d512b87b6e67712fd8bd8de3802688f883600602a0d45b2749089cc.svg) To compute the norm of a multiple of $\mathbf{x},$ say ${\color{blue}-2}\mathbf{x},$ we can factor the absolute value $|{\color{blue}-2}|$ out of the norm as follows:

$$
\begin{aligned}‖-2x‖ & =|-2|\cdot ‖x‖ \\  & =2\cdot \sqrt{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}} \\  & =2\cdot \sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\  & =2\cdot \sqrt{9+16} \\  & =2\cdot \sqrt{25} \\  & =2\cdot 5 \\  & =10.\end{aligned}
$$

**Note:** We can verify that we'd get the same result if we multiplied the $\color{blue}-2$ first, before computing the norm. Multiplying by ${\color{blue}-2},$ we have

![](images/5d/5da07173bda8f167d9a0609457fd10f1e3fdcdd2de498fd08c1c0035a205e5e1.svg)

Then, computing the norm, we get

$$
\begin{aligned}‖-2x‖ & =\sqrt{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}} \\  & =\sqrt{(-6)^{2}+(-8)^{2}} \\  & =\sqrt{36+64} \\  & =\sqrt{100} \\  & =10.\,✓\end{aligned}
$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "128f0f2d-0adc-5542-b2a2-20d7a7ed6223" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3924 :tutorial/title "Factoring a Constant Out of the Norm"} :step/id #uuid "b0b507b6-b42b-5f48-ba2d-8f36454611f9" :step/math-academy-id 23172 :step/next {:db/id 17592186134736}}] :activity/title "The Norm of a Vector in N-Dimensional Euclidean Space" :activity/type {:db/id 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id 17592186133982} {:db/id 17592186134627 :step/content {:db/id 17592186135031 :tutorial/content "The vector space $\mathbb{R}^n,$ paired with the dot product, is called a **Euclidean space**. 

For a vector ![](images/83/838ca0a3284985fa58d5ac894740b15f9bbbfd931299834cc8e062e364bbf983.svg) in $\mathbb{R}^n,$  the **norm** (or **length**)  is given by 

$$
\Vert \mathbf{x} \Vert = \sqrt{\mathbf{x} \cdot \mathbf{x}} = \sqrt{x_1^2+x_2^2+\ldots+x_n^2}.
$$

For example, consider the vector ![](images/2a/2a4e0070bdec6033ab8b71cbb77dbd8569bea8672c0e17cedbf876a77ce56bb2.svg) The norm of this vector is calculated as

$$
\begin{aligned}‖x‖ & =\sqrt{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}} \\  & =\sqrt{(-5)^{2}+(12)^{2}} \\  & =\sqrt{25+144} \\  & =\sqrt{169} \\  & =13.\end{aligned}
$$

Note that the dot product of a vector with itself is always non-negative, $\mathbf{x}\cdot\mathbf{x}\geq 0,$ so it's always possible to take the square root $\sqrt{ \mathbf{x}\cdot\mathbf{x} }.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "cf55fe35-583a-57a7-9a58-529f60897c64" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3602 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "4d2c70c6-8130-51bd-bba0-328eb720e63e" :step/math-academy-id 20183 :step/next {:db/id 17592186134628}} {:db/id 17592186135031 :tutorial/content "The vector space $\mathbb{R}^n,$ paired with the dot product, is called a **Euclidean space**. 

