ademy-id 26194 :step/next {:db/id 17592186158100}} {:db/id 17592186158100 :step/content {:db/id 17592186157273 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186157077 :question/id #uuid "66490eb1-08f7-5df5-9540-88cc538e7d58" :question/math-academy-id "e-7078"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "44bf5c8e-cf2a-595c-b93a-5fe338ffd766" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186157798} {:db/id 17592186157814} {:db/id 17592186375888} {:db/id 17592186377831} {:db/id 17592186387943} {:db/id 17592186394228} {:db/id 17592186417116} {:db/id 17592186417117} {:db/id 17592186417321} {:db/id 17592186417329} {:db/id 17592186506579} {:db/id 17592186506584}] :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying a Statement Using the Distributive Property for Conjunctions"} :step/id #uuid "ee860d46-f0f0-59b9-9a98-146afd430ae9" :step/math-academy-id 26062 :step/next {:db/id 17592186158108}} {:db/id 17592186158108 :step/content {:db/id 17592186157279 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186157083 :question/id #uuid "ef6f99c1-d605-5b09-983d-05837b464e07" :question/math-academy-id "e-7125"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50c12386-8f44-5a50-af97-25cb87ae649f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186157796} {:db/id 17592186157815} {:db/id 17592186375895} {:db/id 17592186375902} {:db/id 17592186387950} {:db/id 17592186394745} {:db/id 17592186417115} {:db/id 17592186417118} {:db/id 17592186417318} {:db/id 17592186417323} {:db/id 17592186417324} {:db/id 17592186506592}] :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying a Statement Using the Distributive Property for Disjunctions"} :step/id #uuid "0df7beb5-c114-51ca-8751-453f137e4362" :step/math-academy-id 26195} {:db/id 17592186158481 :tutorial/content "Just like we can distribute multiplication over addition, we can also distribute conjunctions over disjunctions (and vice versa).

- We can distribute a conjunction over a disjunction, as follows:

$$
A \land (B \lor C)  \equiv (A \land B) \lor (A \land C)
$$

- Likewise, we can distribute a disjunction over a conjunction, as follows:

$$
A \lor (B \land C)  \equiv (A \lor B) \land (A \lor C)
$$

The rules above are called the **distributive laws** for propositional logic. To prove that these laws hold, we can construct truth tables.

- The following truth table shows that $A \land (B \lor C)  \equiv (A \land B) \lor (A \land C){:}$

