![](images/8a/8ab4013ae0d9241268d040220efcd6184e23548e0685802cd6825b59a7bc71b1.svg)

has a solution $x_1 = 1,$ $x_2 = 1,$ and $x_3 = -1,$ which is different from $x_1 = x_2 = x_3 = 0.$ Therefore, we conclude that the vectors are linearly dependent." :tutorial/id #uuid "0d550a61-4219-5ffc-83a2-155ddd8fa9f4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6813 :tutorial/title "Linear Dependence and Independence for a General Set of Vectors"} {:db/id 17592186120881 :step/content {:db/id 17592186121125 :tutorial/content "As with sets containing only two vectors, any set of vectors that contains the zero vector $\mathbf{0}$ must be linearly dependent. 

To see this, consider the set 

$$
\left\{ \mathbf{v}_1, \mathbf{v}_2, \ldots, \mathbf{v}_{n-1}, \mathbf{0} \right\}.
