 Reduction"} :step/id #uuid "4922cdd4-be71-550d-b216-e5a30b232248" :step/math-academy-id 41362} {:activity/expected-seconds 1200.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id 17592186134578} :activity/id #uuid "061434b6-5079-5b3b-b4da-2e73f02d7ea8" :activity/scope {:db/id 17592186050750 :topic/id #uuid "6390206c-1add-5ea0-84f9-8288f56f20b5" :topic/math-academy-id 2163} :activity/steps [{:db/id 17592186134578 :step/content {:db/id 17592186135186 :tutorial/content "The cumulative distribution function (or CDF) of a continuous random variable is defined the same way as for a discrete random variable. 

Given a continuous random variable $X,$ the CDF is the function $F(x)$ such that

$$
F(x) = P(X \leq x).
$$

If our random variable has the probability density function (or PDF) $f(x),$ then we have

$$
F(x) = P(X \leq x) = \int_{-\infty}^x f(t) \, \textrm dt.
$$

Note that we use $f(t)dt$ in the integrand because $x$ serves as the upper limit of integration. 

For example, consider the random variable $X$ with the following PDF:

![](images/e7/e79c230f2b548c9280425f555f6d99a8f997e989289a05fb5f4584b656c4dcc8.svg)

Since $f(x)= 0$ for all $x < 0,$  we have

$$
F(x)  = \int_{-\infty}^x 0 \, \textrm dt = 0
$$

if $x<0.$

To compute the CDF for $x\geq 0,$  we integrate $f(x)$ as follows:

$$
\begin{aligned}F(x) & ={\int }_{-\infty }^{x}f(t)\,\text{d}t \\  & ={\int }_{-\infty }^{0}f(t)\,\text{d}t+{\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\,\text{d}t \\  & =0+{\int }_{0}^{x}\frac{2}{\pi (1+{t}^{2})}\,\text{d}t \\  & ={\frac{2}{\pi }\operatorname{arctan}⁡t|}_{0}^{x} \\  & =\frac{2}{\pi }\operatorname{arctan}⁡x-\frac{2}{\pi }(0) \\  & =\frac{2}{\pi }\operatorname{arctan}⁡x\end{aligned}
$$

Expressing our CDF as a function defined over the entire real line, we get

![](images/66/66b7bf54271075ae72fcf07fa4dc8573a94e97c2b2cc77680c0ec94b6053f360.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "de1bbdf6-687d-537a-a6fe-03d9fafbe841" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8364 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "3f773be7-dfa4-524f-aec7-8ade124c5cea" :step/math-academy-id 132590 :step/next {:db/id 17592186134579}} {:db/id 17592186134579 :step/content {:db/id 17592186133726 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186133516 :question/id #uuid "38b4473a-f956-5af5-863b-387dc10b4924" :question/math-academy-id "e-13362"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d7509fa-a254-5b78-b307-3e0c4ef4d9ec" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id 17592186049638}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186134158} {:db/id 17592186134538} {:db/id 17592186419947} {:db/id 17592186419952} {:db/id 17592186419953} {:db/id 17592186424144} {:db/id 17592186424145}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a CDF Given a PDF With a Lower Bound"} :step/id #uuid "2411aa47-0b30-5e9c-b541-4d44b5f7aeed" :step/math-academy-id 132591 :step/next {:db/id 17592186134580}} {:db/id 17592186134580 :step/content {:db/id 17592186133727 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186133517 :question/id #uuid "98dd834c-ac74-5cdd-88ee-060fd9ac689d" :question/math-academy-id "e-13363"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b0ce833-e045-5aff-94a4-f9ad56cdb904" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186134159} {:db/id 17592186134541} {:db/id 17592186419950} {:db/id 17592186419951} {:db/id 17592186419956} {:db/id 17592186419958} {:db/id 17592186424886} {:db/id 17592186442323} {:db/id 17592186501212} {:db/id 17592186501225}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a CDF Given a PDF That’s Nonzero Over All Real Numbers"} :step/id #uuid "f242a3a5-626f-54b6-b0cc-b8af4506a3f0" :step/math-academy-id 132594 :step/next {:db/id 17592186134581}} {:db/id 17592186134581 :step/content {:db/id 17592186135187 :tutorial/content "When a random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive on the interval $a \leq x \leq b$ only, we have

- $P(X \leq x) = 0$ for $x < a,$ and

- $P(X \leq x) = 1$ for $x > b.$

In these cases, the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:

![](images/7a/7a64bac7c252a3f52fc92f6364edb5c66f31e47da28e78eeb0d069d894135700.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "0794419f-6b72-568b-a787-d71121c8bcd9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8365 :tutorial/title "Finding a CDF Given a PDF With Lower and Upper Bounds"} :step/id #uuid "a74ce991-870f-5bfa-8a96-8bbed06ce5b6" :step/math-academy-id 132597 :step/next {:db/id 17592186134582}} {:db/id 17592186134582 :step/content {:db/id 17592186133728 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186133518 :question/id #uuid "4159a407-ea9f-5728-b219-bf1afab732a2" :question/math-academy-id "e-13364"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eedcfa3d-b02b-5f96-99f4-cd0a452d272a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186134150} {:db/id 17592186134160} {:db/id 17592186419943} {:db/id 17592186419949} {:db/id 17592186419954} {:db/id 17592186419955} {:db/id 17592186424143} {:db/id 17592186424888} {:db/id 17592186442324} {:db/id 17592186442325} {:db/id 17592186500304} {:db/id 17592186501183}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a CDF Given a PDF With Lower and Upper Bounds"} :step/id #uuid "47bc1334-f06b-5cc0-b191-427d1d862db4" :step/math-academy-id 132598 :step/next {:db/id 17592186134583}} {:db/id 17592186134583 :step/content {:db/id 17592186135188 :tutorial/content "As with discrete random variables, we can use the CDF of a continuous random variable to quickly compute a probability over an interval:

$$
P(a \leq X \leq b) = F(b) - F(a)
$$

For example, suppose that a random variable $X$ has the following CDF:

$$
F(x) = \dfrac{1}{1+e^{-x}}, \qquad -\infty < x < \infty.
$$

We can use the above CDF to quickly compute $P(0 \leq x \leq 1),$ as follows:

$$
\begin{aligned}P(0\le x\le 1) & =F(1)-F(0) \\  & =\frac{1}{1+{e}^{-1}}-\frac{1}{1+{e}^{0}} \\  & =\frac{1}{1+{e}^{-1}}-\frac{1}{2} \\  & ≈0.231\end{aligned}
$$

Remember that for continuous random variables, it doesn't matter whether or not we include the endpoints of the interval, so we have

$$
P(0 \leq X \leq 1) = P(0 < X \leq 1) = P(0 \leq X < 1) = P(0 < X < 1) = 0.231.
$$

In general, for a continuous random variable $X,$ we have

$$
P(a \leq X \leq b) = P(a < X \leq b) = P(a \leq X < b) = P(a < X < b) = F(b) - F(a).
$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e5203710-822b-55d4-9dfb-985bc4d41d6a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8366 :tutorial/title "Using a CDF to Compute a Probability Over an Interval"} :step/id #uuid "55418ca8-74f9-5755-9f75-2d411c4a476e" :step/math-academy-id 132601 :step/next {:db/id 17592186134584}} {:db/id 17592186134584 :step/content {:db/id 17592186133729 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186133519 :question/id #uuid "707e3b49-9c13-50d8-8a7f-c4d5ec44e0ff" :question/math-academy-id "e-13365"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3cbc399b-90f3-5acd-ac70-7345a8436978" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186134539} {:db/id 17592186134540} {:db/id 17592186419944} {:db/id 17592186419945} {:db/id 17592186419946} {:db/id 17592186419948} {:db/id 17592186419957} {:db/id 17592186424146} {:db/id 17592186424147} {:db/id 17592186424887} {:db/id 17592186442322} {:db/id 17592186500293}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Over an Interval Using a CDF"} :step/id #uuid "681dc276-df96-5b4d-b26c-338d9da62b88" :step/math-academy-id 132602}] :activity/title "Cumulative Distribution Functions for Continuous Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id 17592186134025} {:db/id 17592186134578 :step/content {:db/id 17592186135186 :tutorial/content "The cumulative distribution function (or CDF) of a continuous random variable is defined the same way as for a discrete random variable. 

