"Removing Discontinuities From Rational Functions"} {:db/id 17592186100803 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186100618 :question/id #uuid "8148cf4c-eae5-54de-adb9-6b8fdf01c7a2" :question/math-academy-id "e-267"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2cd56ebd-30ee-5acf-b691-5c2e5f485b52" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id 17592186049593} {:db/id 17592186049927}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186101438 :question/id #uuid "cccb8d91-fa90-5e8a-84a5-6b526da30fd7" :question/math-academy-id "q-48321"} {:db/id 17592186101440 :question/id #uuid "ab43e107-00fc-5630-8abd-2dc86272117f" :question/math-academy-id "q-48743"}] :knowledge-point/title "Removing Point Discontinuities From Rational Functions"} {:db/id 17592186100618 :question/id #uuid "8148cf4c-eae5-54de-adb9-6b8fdf01c7a2" :question/math-academy-id "e-267" :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id 17592186100803 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2cd56ebd-30ee-5acf-b691-5c2e5f485b52" :knowledge-point/title "Removing Point Discontinuities From Rational Functions"}] :question/problem "Let $g(x)$ be the function defined below. For what value(s) of $k$ is $g(x)$ continuous at $x=-1$?
${\frac{x^{3} + 1}{kx + k},\begin{vmatrix}x \ne - 1 \\ \frac{k}{3},\end{vmatrix}x =-1$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{x^{3} + 1}{kx + k} &= ((x + 1)(x^{2} - x + 1))/(k(x + 1)) \\
&= ((x + 1)(x^{2} - x + 1))/(k(x + 1)) \\
&= \frac{x^{2} - x + 1}{k}
\end{aligned}
$$

Now we can compute the limit at $x=-1$, and get

$$
\lim_\limits{x\to -1} g(x) = \lim_\limits{x\to -1} \left(\dfrac{x^2-x+1}{k}\right) = \dfrac{3}{k}
$$

For $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-1$, we must have

$$
\begin{aligned}
lim_(x → - 1)g(x) &= g(-1) \\
\frac{3}{k} &= \frac{k}{3} \\
9 &= k^{2} \\
± 3 &= k
\end{aligned}
$$

Therefore, for the function to be continuous at $x=-1$, the value of $k$ must be either $-3$ or $3$."} {:db/id 17592186049927} {:db/id 17592186101438 :question/id #uuid "cccb8d91-fa90-5e8a-84a5-6b526da30fd7" :question/math-academy-id "q-48321"} {:db/id 17592186101440 :question/id #uuid "ab43e107-00fc-5630-8abd-2dc86272117f" :question/math-academy-id "q-48743" :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186100803 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2cd56ebd-30ee-5acf-b691-5c2e5f485b52" :knowledge-point/title "Removing Point Discontinuities From Rational Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f2d497f-046e-5ff8-9cc4-36ed95644117" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No such $k$ exists" :db/id 17592186099802} {:answer/id #uuid "932f68d0-3981-5043-bca8-0d34e2a7c58c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "± 3" :db/id 17592186099803} {:answer/id #uuid "b489cad0-a823-5608-8178-c647f5a4a9d1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "± 2" :db/id 17592186099804} {:answer/id #uuid "139fc8af-4064-5873-aff1-d72d9a72cf0b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "± 5" :db/id 17592186099805} {:answer/id #uuid "200d7f0a-daed-5888-8a11-0597758e9c97" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "± 1" :db/id 17592186099806}] :answer-field/id #uuid "81d7abef-1e5f-5e7e-a713-da16f17f6fd9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186099801}] :question/problem "Let $g(x)$ be the function defined below. For what values of $k$ is $g(x)$ continuous at $x =-2$?
$g(x) = {\frac{x^{2} + 5x + 6}{kx + 2k},\begin{vmatrix}x \ne - 2 \\ k,\end{vmatrix}x =-2$"} {:db/id 17592186100804 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186100619 :question/id #uuid "837cbd93-6e15-537c-8661-37f42294e66f" :question/math-academy-id "e-268"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "583e8820-a8c9-5c94-84cc-d5ff1ee239b4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id 17592186048741} {:db/id 17592186048774} {:db/id 17592186048784} {:db/id 17592186050511}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186101441 :question/id #uuid "9e59f2aa-e25b-5487-8fcd-421c139ce6e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-48752"} {:db/id 17592186101443 :question/id #uuid "09ee0fec-50b2-5471-9144-5d95050e6a7c" :question/math-academy-id "q-48885"} {:db/id 17592186234014 :question/id #uuid "d3161c6d-15b1-5502-a086-55781c56e488" :question/math-academy-id "q-38592"}] :knowledge-point/title "Removing Jump Discontinuities From Rational Functions"} {:db/id 17592186100619 :question/id #uuid "837cbd93-6e15-537c-8661-37f42294e66f" :question/math-academy-id "e-268" :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id 17592186100804 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "583e8820-a8c9-5c94-84cc-d5ff1ee239b4" :knowledge-point/title "Removing Jump Discontinuities From Rational Functions"}] :question/problem "Let $f(x)$ be the function defined below. Find the values of $k$ for which $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2$.
${\frac{x^{2} + 4x - 12}{x^{2} - 4},\begin{vmatrix}x < 2 \\ 2^{kx},\end{vmatrix}x \ge 2$." :question/worked-solution "From the definition of $f(x)$, the left-sided limit of $f(x)$ at $x=2$ is

$$
\begin{aligned}
lim_(x → 2^{-})f(x) &= lim_(x → 2^{-})\frac{x^{2} + 4x - 12}{x^{2} - 4} \\
&= lim_(x → 2^{-})((x - 2)(x + 6))/((x - 2)(x + 2)) \\
&= lim_(x → 2^{-})((x - 2)(x + 6))/((x - 2)(x + 2)) \\
&= lim_(x → 2^{-})\frac{x + 6}{x + 2} \\
&= \frac{2 + 6}{2 + 2} \\
&= 2
\end{aligned}
$$

On the other hand, the right-sided limit is

$$
\begin{aligned}
lim_(x → 2^{+})f(x) &= lim_(x → 2^{+})2^{kx} \\
&= 2^{2k}
\end{aligned}
$$

We also note that

$$
f(2) = 2^{2k}
$$

So, for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2$, we must have

$$
\begin{aligned}
lim_(x → 2^{+})f(x) &= lim_(x → 2^{-})f(x) \\
2^{2k} &= 2 \\
2^{2k} &= 2^{1} \\
2k &= 1 \\
k &= \frac{1}{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

Therefore,

$$
k=\dfrac12
$$

removes the discontinuity at $x=2$."} {:db/id 17592186050511} {:db/id 17592186101441 :question/id #uuid "9e59f2aa-e25b-5487-8fcd-421c139ce6e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-48752"} {:db/id 17592186101443 :question/id #uuid "09ee0fec-50b2-5471-9144-5d95050e6a7c" :question/math-academy-id "q-48885"} {:db/id 17592186234014 :question/id #uuid "d3161c6d-15b1-5502-a086-55781c56e488" :question/math-academy-id "q-38592"} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f2d497f-046e-5ff8-9cc4-36ed95644117" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No such $k$ exists" :db/id 17592186099802} {:answer/id #uuid "932f68d0-3981-5043-bca8-0d34e2a7c58c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "± 3" :db/id 17592186099803} {:answer/id #uuid "b489cad0-a823-5608-8178-c647f5a4a9d1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "± 2" :db/id 17592186099804} {:answer/id #uuid "139fc8af-4064-5873-aff1-d72d9a72cf0b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "± 5" :db/id 17592186099805} {:answer/id #uuid "200d7f0a-daed-5888-8a11-0597758e9c97" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "± 1" :db/id 17592186099806}] :answer-field/id #uuid "81d7abef-1e5f-5e7e-a713-da16f17f6fd9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186099801} {:answer/id #uuid "8f2d497f-046e-5ff8-9cc4-36ed95644117" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No such $k$ exists" :db/id 17592186099802} {:answer/id #uuid "932f68d0-3981-5043-bca8-0d34e2a7c58c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "± 3" :db/id 17592186099803} {:answer/id #uuid "b489cad0-a823-5608-8178-c647f5a4a9d1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "± 2" :db/id 17592186099804} {:answer/id #uuid "139fc8af-4064-5873-aff1-d72d9a72cf0b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "± 5" :db/id 17592186099805} {:answer/id #uuid "200d7f0a-daed-5888-8a11-0597758e9c97" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "± 1" :db/id 17592186099806} {:activity/first-step {:db/id 17592186101570} :activity/id #uuid "4b7be0a1-49cf-57e8-8286-c52f0151df07" :activity/scope {:db/id 17592186049825 :topic/id #uuid "16e6877f-e43f-5432-a6fb-d2029db06718" :topic/math-academy-id 1225} :activity/steps [{:db/id 17592186101570 :step/content {:db/id 17592186101995 :tutorial/content "Consider the function $f(x)$ below:

$$
{\frac{x^{2} - 1}{x^{2} + x - 2},\begin{vmatrix}x \ne 1 \\ k,\end{vmatrix}x = 1
$$

The function $f(x)$ seems to have a point discontinuity at $x=1$. However, by selecting a particular value for $k$, we can remove the point discontinuity and make the function continuous.

We can find this value of $k$ by forcing the limit of the function to be equal to the value of the function at $x=1$:

$$
\lim\limits_{x\to 1} f(x) = f(1)
$$

Remember that $\lim\limits_{x\to 1} f(x)$ represents the value that $f(x)$ approaches as $x$ gets closer and closer, but not equal, to $1$. So, when evaluating $\lim\limits_{x\to 1} f(x)$, we need to use the function definition for

$$
x \neq 1
$$

$$
\begin{aligned}
lim_(x → 1)f(x) &= lim_(x → 1)\frac{x^{2} - 1}{x^{2} + x - 2} \\
&= lim_(x → 1)((x - 1)(x + 1))/((x - 1)(x + 2)) \\
&= lim_(x → 1)((x - 1)(x + 1))/((x - 1)(x + 2)) \\
&= lim_(x → 1)\frac{x + 1}{x + 2} \\
&= \frac{1 + 1}{1 + 2} \\
&= \frac{2}{3}
\end{aligned}
$$

At $x=1$, the function is defined as

$$
f(x)=k
$$

So,

$$
f(1)=k
$$

, and for continuity we must have

$$
\begin{aligned}
lim_(x → 1)f(x) &= f(1) \\
\frac{2}{3} &= k
\end{aligned}
$$

Therefore, to make $f(x)$ continuous at $x=1$, we need to choose

$$
k=\dfrac{2}{3}
$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "81a7db1c-98c9-5308-96d8-457b407671db" :tutorial/math-academy-id 548 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "d10fc2dc-3be0-5724-8892-7273ab4b7322" :step/math-academy-id 1031 :step/next {:db/id 17592186101571}} {:db/id 17592186101571 :step/content {:db/id 17592186100803 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186100618 :question/id #uuid "8148cf4c-eae5-54de-adb9-6b8fdf01c7a2" :question/math-academy-id "e-267"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2cd56ebd-30ee-5acf-b691-5c2e5f485b52" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id 17592186049593} {:db/id 17592186049927}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186101438} {:db/id 17592186101440}] :knowledge-point/title "Removing Point Discontinuities From Rational Functions"} :step/id #uuid "080a88b1-8b9c-503a-a435-862f18fa647f" :step/math-academy-id 1032 :step/next {:db/id 17592186101572}} {:db/id 17592186101572 :step/content {:db/id 17592186100804 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186100619 :question/id #uuid "837cbd93-6e15-537c-8661-37f42294e66f" :question/math-academy-id "e-268"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "583e8820-a8c9-5c94-84cc-d5ff1ee239b4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id 17592186048741} {:db/id 17592186048774} {:db/id 17592186048784} {:db/id 17592186050511}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186101441} {:db/id 17592186101443} {:db/id 17592186234014}] :knowledge-point/title "Removing Jump Discontinuities From Rational Functions"} :step/id #uuid "7867d9d8-2d45-52ba-b25f-cdcdfeac2305" :step/math-academy-id 1035}] :activity/title "Removing Discontinuities From Rational Functions" :activity/type {:db/id 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id 17592186101077} {:db/id 17592186101570 :step/content {:db/id 17592186101995 :tutorial/content "Consider the function $f(x)$ below:

$$
{\frac{x^{2} - 1}{x^{2} + x - 2},\begin{vmatrix}x \ne 1 \\ k,\end{vmatrix}x = 1
$$

The function $f(x)$ seems to have a point discontinuity at $x=1$. However, by selecting a particular value for $k$, we can remove the point discontinuity and make the function continuous.

We can find this value of $k$ by forcing the limit of the function to be equal to the value of the function at $x=1$:

$$
\lim\limits_{x\to 1} f(x) = f(1)
$$

Remember that $\lim\limits_{x\to 1} f(x)$ represents the value that $f(x)$ approaches as $x$ gets closer and closer, but not equal, to $1$. So, when evaluating $\lim\limits_{x\to 1} f(x)$, we need to use the function definition for

$$
x \neq 1
$$

$$
\begin{aligned}
lim_(x → 1)f(x) &= lim_(x → 1)\frac{x^{2} - 1}{x^{2} + x - 2} \\
&= lim_(x → 1)((x - 1)(x + 1))/((x - 1)(x + 2)) \\
&= lim_(x → 1)((x - 1)(x + 1))/((x - 1)(x + 2)) \\
&= lim_(x → 1)\frac{x + 1}{x + 2} \\
&= \frac{1 + 1}{1 + 2} \\
&= \frac{2}{3}
\end{aligned}
$$

At $x=1$, the function is defined as

$$
f(x)=k
$$

So,

$$
f(1)=k
$$

, and for continuity we must have

$$
\begin{aligned}
lim_(x → 1)f(x) &= f(1) \\
\frac{2}{3} &= k
\end{aligned}
$$

Therefore, to make $f(x)$ continuous at $x=1$, we need to choose

$$
k=\dfrac{2}{3}
$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "81a7db1c-98c9-5308-96d8-457b407671db" :tutorial/math-academy-id 548 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "d10fc2dc-3be0-5724-8892-7273ab4b7322" :step/math-academy-id 1031 :step/next {:db/id 17592186101571}} {:db/id 17592186101995 :tutorial/content "Consider the function $f(x)$ below:

