determine the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about ![](images/0d/0de380d24ed675cff86b219a2a4a337699d68e168fc875475158523022915b0c.svg) Give your answer in matrix form." :question/worked-solution "Suppose that the function $\mathbf{f}:\mathbb R^n\to\mathbb R$ is defined by 

![](images/61/61c5a4204c4ad078bd036eee7770e28faf4741bedb274a54d08d54dae43fbc68.svg) 

and is twice differentiable at $\mathbf x = \mathbf a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $\mathbf a$ is given by

$$
\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\end{aligned}
$$

where

![](images/ab/ab531b6a5468a0ddb8cf46a42300f19cc6406c94c011ae814d60af24f66e2aa0.svg)

and

![](images/3d/3dbb0d517f6875d4a0601ba284878cb60ac0b615491564ae9458c34c0f3de403.svg)

In this case, we have the following:

$$
\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\begin{bmatrix}\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \frac{∂f}{∂y}\end{bmatrix}, & \,{f}^{″}(x) & =\begin{bmatrix}\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\ \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\end{bmatrix}\end{aligned}
$$

Now, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial:

- Calculating the value of the function, we have

$$
\begin{aligned}f(1,1) & =0.\end{aligned}
$$

- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:

$$
\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\begin{bmatrix}2xy-{y}^{3} & {x}^{2}-3x{y}^{2}\end{bmatrix} \\ {f}^{′}(1,1) & =\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2\end{bmatrix}\end{aligned}
$$

- Calculating the second derivative, we have the following:

$$
\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =\begin{bmatrix}2y & 2x-3{y}^{2} \\ 2x-3{y}^{2} & -6xy\end{bmatrix} \\ {f}^{″}(1,1) & =\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\ -1 & -6\end{bmatrix}\end{aligned}
$$

Therefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial (in matrix form) is given by

![](images/3c/3cbbec95c6327e77a53bdeea5de4a93481ca9f4164ebae0cb99bf28468513a12.svg)"} {:db/id 17592186198637 :question/id #uuid "6d7c182a-d8d9-5d67-83bd-5c70deb44da7" :question/math-academy-id "q-162947" :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48adeec5-fd51-5fd0-9cbb-865947abcfc9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+3" :db/id 17592186432346} {:answer/id #uuid "603d35dd-8efa-5544-b92b-f29e859ccbb6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-1" :db/id 17592186432348} {:answer/id #uuid "b5fee454-4dd2-565f-bb9f-e0034cd35e1c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+3" :db/id 17592186432356} {:answer/id #uuid "c0760b03-d85d-5f8d-87d7-29831c845c02" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+1" :db/id 17592186432358} {:answer/id #uuid "d4bba0dc-736f-564b-a952-443458426144" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x-2" :db/id 17592186432359}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id 17592186432358} :answer-field/id #uuid "d001a817-7196-53c1-ab12-d39da924742e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493} :db/id 17592186194453}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487} :question/problem "For the function $f:{R}^{2}\to R,$ defined as

$$
f(x,y)={x}^{3}y+x{y}^{3}+{y}^{2},
$$

the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=\begin{bmatrix}1 \\ 0\end{bmatrix}$ is given by

$$
f(x,y)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\frac{1}{2}{\begin{bmatrix}∗ \\ y\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\begin{bmatrix}0 & ∗ \\ 3 & 2\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}∗ \\ y\end{bmatrix}.
$$

Find the sum of the three missing expressions." :question/worked-solution "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\to R$ is defined by 

$$
f:\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\ {x}_{2} \\ ⋮ \\ {x}_{n}\end{bmatrix}⟶z
$$



and is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by

$$
\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\end{aligned}
$$

where

$$
{f}^{′}(a)={\begin{bmatrix}\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\end{bmatrix}|}_{x=a}
$$

and

$$
{f}^{″}(a)={\begin{bmatrix}\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\ \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\ \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\end{bmatrix}}_{x=a}
$$

In this case, we have the following:

$$
\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\begin{bmatrix}\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \frac{∂f}{∂y}\end{bmatrix}, & \,{f}^{″}(x) & =\begin{bmatrix}\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\ \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\end{bmatrix}\end{aligned}
$$

Calculating the first derivative, we have

$$
\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\begin{bmatrix}3{x}^{2}y+{y}^{3} & {x}^{3}+3x{y}^{2}+2y\end{bmatrix}.\end{aligned}
$$

Calculating the second derivative, we have the following:

$$
\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =\begin{bmatrix}6xy & 3{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2} \\ 3{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2} & 6xy+2\end{bmatrix} \\ {f}^{″}(1,0) & =\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\ 3 & 2\end{bmatrix}\end{aligned}
$$

Therefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by

$$
f(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\frac{1}{2}{\begin{bmatrix}x-1 \\ y\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\ 3 & 2\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}x-1 \\ y\end{bmatrix}.
$$

Finally, the sum of the missing expressions is

$$
(x-1)+3+(x-1)=2x+1.
$$"} {:db/id 17592186198643 :question/id #uuid "2ec5ff3d-fd4d-5171-8304-f2a9e9fbb4f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-162989"} {:db/id 17592186281602 :question/id #uuid "c7c75fe7-9dcd-544f-9c7d-b36cf94f46b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-163102" :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186198435 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b91ae708-a63d-51ac-9ae0-f24724392858" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Taylor Polynomial in Matrix Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06d21ce0-309e-51b2-b71a-317f7673986c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x" :db/id 17592186278656} {:answer/id #uuid "3af19d5c-b69f-565b-999b-0e2e1458f0dc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+3" :db/id 17592186279115} {:answer/id #uuid "9e74d61d-582b-5a16-ab29-864d8dbb15a2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x" :db/id 17592186279997} {:answer/id #uuid "abf5d547-610e-59e8-93d7-5d6cc804f793" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+1" :db/id 17592186280116} {:answer/id #uuid "cce87375-9ff6-5f1f-b864-2b4877a1fcfb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-1" :db/id 17592186280394}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e74d61d-582b-5a16-ab29-864d8dbb15a2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x" :db/id 17592186279997} :answer-field/id #uuid "70d1b0f9-cfc4-562a-82ed-b91bc6be2331" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186281031}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/problem "For the function $f:{R}^{2}\to R,$ defined as

