$$
f:\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\ {x}_{2} \\ ⋮ \\ {x}_{n}\end{bmatrix}⟶z
$$



and is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by

$$
\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\end{aligned}
$$

where

$$
{f}^{′}(a)={\begin{bmatrix}\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\end{bmatrix}|}_{x=a}
$$

and

$$
{f}^{″}(a)={\begin{bmatrix}\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\ \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\ \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\end{bmatrix}}_{x=a}
$$

In this case, we have the following:

$$
\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\begin{bmatrix}\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \frac{∂f}{∂y}\end{bmatrix}, & \,{f}^{″}(x) & =\begin{bmatrix}\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\ \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\end{bmatrix}\end{aligned}
$$

Calculating the first derivative, we have

$$
\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\begin{bmatrix}\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\operatorname{cos}⁡x & \operatorname{sin}⁡x-x\operatorname{sin}⁡y\end{bmatrix}.\end{aligned}
$$

Calculating the second derivative, we have the following:

$$
\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =\begin{bmatrix}-y\operatorname{sin}⁡x & \operatorname{cos}⁡x-\operatorname{sin}⁡y \\ \operatorname{cos}⁡x-\operatorname{sin}⁡y & -x\operatorname{cos}⁡y\end{bmatrix} \\ {f}^{″}(\frac{\pi }{2},0) & =\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\ 0 & -\frac{\pi }{2}\end{bmatrix}\end{aligned}
$$

Therefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by

$$
f(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\frac{1}{2}{\begin{bmatrix}x-\frac{\pi }{2} \\ y\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\ 0 & -\frac{\pi }{2}\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}x-\frac{\pi }{2} \\ y\end{bmatrix}.
$$

Finally, the sum of the missing expressions is

$$
(x-\frac{\pi }{2})+(-\frac{\pi }{2})+(x-\frac{\pi }{2})=2x-\frac{3\pi }{2}.
$$"} {:db/id 17592186281608 :question/id #uuid "2c3dba84-c7b2-5a37-bb07-b58c80773105" :question/math-academy-id "q-163142"} {:db/id 17592186281609 :question/id #uuid "f0b608b5-0740-5c5c-a8d8-286478d0cfd4" :question/math-academy-id "q-163143" :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186198435 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b91ae708-a63d-51ac-9ae0-f24724392858" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Taylor Polynomial in Matrix Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0889e045-221d-548d-8586-c263b14daf17" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y-2" :db/id 17592186278673} {:answer/id #uuid "36bda793-84bc-523f-874b-ecf8461dfe2c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3y+7" :db/id 17592186279071} {:answer/id #uuid "b804339b-81d0-5458-8b89-1119bfccd852" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3y-3" :db/id 17592186280210} {:answer/id #uuid "bec80d1d-dc4a-5247-8229-4f3da550f0c2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3y+10" :db/id 17592186280269} {:answer/id #uuid "ccd330d2-e165-5873-970a-d719dc56d528" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y+8" :db/id 17592186280392}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36bda793-84bc-523f-874b-ecf8461dfe2c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3y+7" :db/id 17592186279071} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd3902cd-4ea0-53f7-8e46-8bbcf944e194" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186281220}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/problem "For the function $f:{R}^{2}\to R,$ defined as

