Finally, the sum of the missing expressions is

$$
e+3e+(y-2)+(y-2)+9e+(-3e)=2y+10e-4.
$$"} {:db/id 17592186367811 :question/id #uuid "cdc26b29-0619-5b64-aa82-450f2b706603" :question/math-academy-id "q-163106" :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40a5e6d9-c4fd-57bb-8147-cf98861b9d12" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-3x" :db/id 17592186367813} {:answer/id #uuid "1719c348-9c4f-5f66-9ae0-a4eafaef1b2c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "2-x" :db/id 17592186367814} {:answer/id #uuid "e8f81270-21c7-5914-a3c9-3982421e3eaf" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-1" :db/id 17592186367815} {:answer/id #uuid "f5eb386a-91a6-5494-94a1-33f62862e3cb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "x" :db/id 17592186367816} {:answer/id #uuid "7bb293b3-3530-5187-923a-dc9dda0e6dc1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x-3" :db/id 17592186367817}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id 17592186367817} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a4e6984-cf2e-55cb-9a10-debfbaccb6f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493} :db/id 17592186367812}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487} :question/problem "For the function $f:{R}^{2}\to R,$ defined as

$$
f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{e}^{x-y},
$$

the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=\begin{bmatrix}1 \\ 1\end{bmatrix}$ is given by

$$
f(x,y)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\frac{1}{2}{\begin{bmatrix}∗ \\ y-1\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\begin{bmatrix}3 & ∗ \\ -1 & 1\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}∗ \\ y-1\end{bmatrix}.
$$

Find the sum of the three missing expressions." :question/worked-solution "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\to R$ is defined by 

$$
f:\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\ {x}_{2} \\ ⋮ \\ {x}_{n}\end{bmatrix}⟶z
$$



and is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by

$$
\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\end{aligned}
$$

where

$$
{f}^{′}(a)={\begin{bmatrix}\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\end{bmatrix}|}_{x=a}
$$

and

$$
{f}^{″}(a)={\begin{bmatrix}\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\ \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\ \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\end{bmatrix}}_{x=a}
$$

In this case, we have the following:

$$
\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\begin{bmatrix}\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \frac{∂f}{∂y}\end{bmatrix}, & \,{f}^{″}(x) & =\begin{bmatrix}\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\ \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\end{bmatrix}\end{aligned}
$$

Calculating the first derivative, we have

$$
\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\begin{bmatrix}2x+{e}^{x-y} & -{e}^{x-y}\end{bmatrix}.\end{aligned}
$$

Calculating the second derivative, we have the following:

$$
\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =\begin{bmatrix}2+{e}^{x-y} & -{e}^{x-y} \\ -{e}^{x-y} & {e}^{x-y}\end{bmatrix} \\ {f}^{″}(1,1) & =\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\ -1 & 1\end{bmatrix}\end{aligned}
$$

Therefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by

$$
f(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\frac{1}{2}{\begin{bmatrix}x-1 \\ y-1\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\ -1 & 1\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}x-1 \\ y-1\end{bmatrix}.
$$

Finally, the sum of the missing expressions is

$$
(x-1)+(-1)+(x-1)=2x-3.
$$"} {:db/id 17592186367825 :question/id #uuid "45c5b66d-4dfc-59b3-9af3-6cbfa7931243" :question/math-academy-id "q-163123"} {:db/id 17592186432372 :question/id #uuid "c474ae22-f722-549c-93e7-7a344f22e1ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-163127" :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186198435 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b91ae708-a63d-51ac-9ae0-f24724392858" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Taylor Polynomial in Matrix Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1655fe4a-7777-5f50-b20e-15de50423a6d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+14" :db/id 17592186432338} {:answer/id #uuid "2b681e68-e901-5719-ac47-b49d4ce4701f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+9" :db/id 17592186432341} {:answer/id #uuid "7d832a86-9407-57b1-9b8a-27976545a36c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-5" :db/id 17592186432351} {:answer/id #uuid "a4e42428-9e42-5652-97ed-add0db5753d1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+12" :db/id 17592186432354} {:answer/id #uuid "feef8743-284c-59a2-9f3c-29febdcbdf54" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x-2" :db/id 17592186432364}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1655fe4a-7777-5f50-b20e-15de50423a6d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+14" :db/id 17592186432338} :answer-field/id #uuid "67008e00-3fcb-5aa6-b40b-a1c4a9012f7f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186432367}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/problem "For the function $f:{R}^{2}\to R,$ defined as

$$
f(x,y)=3x+2y+{x}^{2}{y}^{3},
$$

the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=\begin{bmatrix}1 \\ 1\end{bmatrix}$ is given by

$$
f(x)≈∗+\begin{bmatrix}5 & ∗\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}∗ \\ y-1\end{bmatrix}+\frac{1}{2}{\begin{bmatrix}∗ \\ y-1\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 \\ 6 & ∗\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}∗ \\ y-1\end{bmatrix}.
$$

Find the sum of the six missing expressions." :question/worked-solution "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\to R$ is defened by 

$$
f:\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\ {x}_{2} \\ ⋮ \\ {x}_{n}\end{bmatrix}⟶z
$$



and is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by

$$
\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\end{aligned}
$$

where

$$
{f}^{′}(a)={\begin{bmatrix}\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\end{bmatrix}|}_{x=a}
$$

and

$$
{f}^{″}(a)={\begin{bmatrix}\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\ \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\ \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\end{bmatrix}}_{x=a}
$$

In this case, we have the following:

$$
\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\begin{bmatrix}\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \frac{∂f}{∂y}\end{bmatrix}, & \,{f}^{″}(x) & =\begin{bmatrix}\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\ \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\end{bmatrix}\end{aligned}
$$

Now, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial:

- Calculating the value of the function, we have

$$
\begin{aligned}f(1,1) & =6.\end{aligned}
$$

- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:

$$
\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\begin{bmatrix}3+2x{y}^{3} & 2+3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\end{bmatrix} \\ {f}^{′}(1,1) & =\begin{bmatrix}5 & 5\end{bmatrix}\end{aligned}
$$

- Calculating the second derivative, we have the following:

$$
\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =\begin{bmatrix}2{y}^{3} & 6x{y}^{2} \\ 6x{y}^{2} & 6{x}^{2}y\end{bmatrix} \\ {f}^{″}(1,1) & =\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 \\ 6 & 6\end{bmatrix}\end{aligned}
$$

Therefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by

$$
f(x)≈6+\begin{bmatrix}5 & 5\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}x-1 \\ y-1\end{bmatrix}+\frac{1}{2}{\begin{bmatrix}x-1 \\ y-1\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 \\ 6 & 6\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}x-1 \\ y-1\end{bmatrix}
$$

Finally, the sum of the missing expressions is

$$
6+5+(x-1)+(x-1)+6+(x-1)=3x+14.
$$"} {:db/id 17592186198436 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186198078 :question/id #uuid "137d7caf-b232-5e21-9583-14d907a98624" :question/math-academy-id "e-12564"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b19cc766-bdc5-5ab2-aa4d-ebddf8311116" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186198678 :question/id #uuid "a0ff3e7e-07e5-5c92-aabb-370f248ce704" :question/math-academy-id "q-164611"} {:db/id 17592186198679 :question/id #uuid "7fcb7ddf-9df6-5eb3-88f6-02683fa452c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-164809"} {:db/id 17592186284521 :question/id #uuid "f42addea-156e-5dc2-b6d9-50bdb56ae67d" :question/math-academy-id "q-164827"} {:db/id 17592186284522 :question/id #uuid "e72817fe-f751-5c2a-9faa-b82f2365cc69" :question/math-academy-id "q-164828"} {:db/id 17592186284523 :question/id #uuid "af702ba5-d262-51dd-8530-cde831134efb" :question/math-academy-id "q-164830"} {:db/id 17592186284524 :question/id #uuid "47346af3-1988-59d6-b35f-cf2ca4f49c94" :question/math-academy-id "q-164831"} {:db/id 17592186284530 :question/id #uuid "6aa91597-6ee2-53be-bdf3-8769a0188799" :question/math-academy-id "q-164858"} {:db/id 17592186284531 :question/id #uuid "476ff359-d053-5b12-8e13-351e9dc83e70" :question/math-academy-id "q-164859"} {:db/id 17592186367901 :question/id #uuid "34533486-cd0d-5205-b2b8-1ba449843c04" :question/math-academy-id "q-164861"} {:db/id 17592186367908 :question/id #uuid "a2bdcbd2-3eb2-5c14-bb14-2f06b27bbd7b" :question/math-academy-id "q-164862"} {:db/id 17592186432371 :question/id #uuid "86a1a7c8-4805-516c-8e49-de078c8ddcd1" :question/math-academy-id "q-164829"} {:db/id 17592186432373 :question/id #uuid "e65d6e32-4824-5250-9bff-fdfee93e977a" :question/math-academy-id "q-164860"}] :knowledge-point/title "Approximating a Multivariable Function at a Point Using a Quadratic Taylor Polynomial"} {:db/id 17592186198078 :question/id #uuid "137d7caf-b232-5e21-9583-14d907a98624" :question/math-academy-id "e-12564" :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id 17592186198436 :knowled