$$

Use the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=\begin{bmatrix}1 \\ 0\end{bmatrix}$ to approximate the value of $f(1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\to R$ is defined by 

$$
f:\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\ {x}_{2} \\ ⋮ \\ {x}_{n}\end{bmatrix}⟶z
$$



and is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by

$$
\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\end{aligned}
$$

where

$$
{f}^{′}(a)={\begin{bmatrix}\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\end{bmatrix}|}_{x=a}
$$

and

$$
{f}^{″}(a)={\begin{bmatrix}\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\ \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\ \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\end{bmatrix}}_{x=a}
$$

In this case, we have $a=\begin{bmatrix}1 \\ 0\end{bmatrix},$ and the following data:

$$
\begin{aligned}f(a) & =3, & \,{f}^{′}(a) & =\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1\end{bmatrix}, & \,{f}^{″}(a) & =\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\ 1 & 1\end{bmatrix}\end{aligned}
$$

Therefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by

$$
f(x)≈3+\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}x-1 \\ y\end{bmatrix}+\frac{1}{2}{\begin{bmatrix}x-1 \\ y\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\ 1 & 1\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}x-1 \\ y\end{bmatrix}
$$

Finally, evaluating the polynomial at $x=\begin{bmatrix}1 \\ 2\end{bmatrix},$ we obtain

$$
\begin{aligned}f(1,2) & ≈3+\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}1-1 \\ 2\end{bmatrix}+\frac{1}{2}{\begin{bmatrix}1-1 \\ 2\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\ 1 & 1\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}1-1 \\ 2\end{bmatrix} \\  & =3+\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}0 \\ 2\end{bmatrix}+\frac{1}{2}{\begin{bmatrix}0 \\ 2\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\ 1 & 1\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}0 \\ 2\end{bmatrix} \\  & =3+(-2)+\frac{1}{2}\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}2 \\ 2\end{bmatrix} \\  & =1+\frac{1}{2}(4) \\  & =3.\end{aligned}
