Use the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\ 0\end{bmatrix}$ to approximate the value of $f(-2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\to R$ is defined by 

$$
f:\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\ {x}_{2} \\ ⋮ \\ {x}_{n}\end{bmatrix}⟶z
$$



and is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by

$$
\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\end{aligned}
$$

where

$$
{f}^{′}(a)={\begin{bmatrix}\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\end{bmatrix}|}_{x=a}
$$

and

$$
{f}^{″}(a)={\begin{bmatrix}\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\ \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\ \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\end{bmatrix}}_{x=a}
$$

In this case, we have $a=\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\ 0\end{bmatrix},$ and the following data:

$$
\begin{aligned}f(a) & =0, & \,{f}^{′}(a) & =\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0\end{bmatrix}, & \,{f}^{″}(a) & =\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\ 2 & 1\end{bmatrix}\end{aligned}
$$

Therefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by

$$
f(x)≈0+\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}x+1 \\ y\end{bmatrix}+\frac{1}{2}{\begin{bmatrix}x+1 \\ y\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\ 2 & 1\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}x+1 \\ y\end{bmatrix}
$$

Finally, evaluating the polynomial at $x=\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\ 1\end{bmatrix},$ we obtain

$$
\begin{aligned}f(-2,1) & ≈0+\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}-2+1 \\ 1\end{bmatrix}+\frac{1}{2}{\begin{bmatrix}-2+1 \\ 1\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\ 2 & 1\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}-2+1 \\ 1\end{bmatrix} \\  & =0+\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\ 1\end{bmatrix}+\frac{1}{2}{\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\ 1\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\ 2 & 1\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\ 1\end{bmatrix} \\  & =0+(-1)+\frac{1}{2}\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}3 \\ -1\end{bmatrix} \\  & =-1+\frac{1}{2}(-4) \\  & =-3.\end{aligned}
$$"} {:db/id 17592186432373 :question/id #uuid "e65d6e32-4824-5250-9bff-fdfee93e977a" :question/math-academy-id "q-164860" :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186198436 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b19cc766-bdc5-5ab2-aa4d-ebddf8311116" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating a Multivariable Function at a Point Using a Quadratic Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32b5cfc5-5b79-5869-9762-17001e7c7019" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id 17592186432342} {:answer/id #uuid "41ed0217-d425-5f4e-ba99-e0ac900da7d6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id 17592186432343} {:answer/id #uuid "46fa0700-2e70-5cfa-8b00-54b4c7338732" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\frac{7}{2}" :db/id 17592186432345} {:answer/id #uuid "ad9aead5-698e-55fd-a7ed-c0810fdc4083" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id 17592186432355} {:answer/id #uuid "f2ec6153-7a5a-564c-b65e-6663c5889657" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\frac{9}{2}" :db/id 17592186432361}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46fa0700-2e70-5cfa-8b00-54b4c7338732" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\frac{7}{2}" :db/id 17592186432345} :answer-field/id #uuid "34ace458-f233-535c-b685-fcd05334410a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186432366}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/problem "For the twice-differentiable function $f:{R}^{3}\to R,$ we have the following data:

