id #uuid "ade971db-c5ab-5cf8-bede-d4db99d2b5f8" :step/math-academy-id 2656 :step/next {:db/id 17592186106752}} {:db/id 17592186106752 :step/content {:db/id 17592186105813 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186105678 :question/id #uuid "4228f47a-3a9b-54e4-82a6-b72837039d70" :question/math-academy-id "e-779"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "969d32c6-b343-5e1f-9521-94ff346645b3" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id 17592186050323}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186106445} {:db/id 17592186106448} {:db/id 17592186233097}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Correct Limits of a Given Function when Simplification is Required"} :step/id #uuid "c44388fb-c586-558c-b35f-1e1ef27bb6fd" :step/math-academy-id 2660}] :activity/title "Connecting Infinite Limits and Vertical Asymptotes of Rational Functions" :activity/type {:db/id 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id 17592186106008} {:db/id 17592186106748 :step/content {:db/id 17592186107031 :tutorial/content "Rational functions have infinite limits when the $x$-value approaches vertical asymptotes. To find the infinite limits of a rational function $f(x)$, we first find the vertical asymptotes.

For example, consider the rational function

$$
f(x) = \dfrac{1}{x-2}
$$

This rational function has a vertical asymptote at $x=2$.

To find the one-sided limits at the asymptote $x=2$, we check the sign of $f(x)$ as $x$ approaches $2$ from the left and the right.

- To find the limit as $x \to 2^-$, we evaluate $f(x)$ at $x$-values very close to $x=2$ from the left:

| $x$ | $1.9$ | $1.99$ | $1.999$ |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| $f(x)$ | $-10$ | $-100$ | $-1\,000$ |

The values of $f(x)$ are negative, and they get more and more negative. Therefore,

$$
\lim\limits_{x \to 2^-} f(x) = -\infty
$$

- To find the limit as $x \to 2^+$, we evaluate $f(x)$ at $x$-values very close to $x=2$ from the right:

| $x$ | $2.1$ | $2.01$ | $2.001$ |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| $f(x)$ | $10$ | $100$ | $1\,000$ |

The values of $f(x)$ are positive, and they get larger and larger. Therefore,

$$
\lim\limits_{x \to 2^+} f(x) = +\infty
$$

These one-sided limits match up with what we see in the graph of $f(x)$, shown below.

