dent :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186259479}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/problem "![](images/5b/5b6490495c297b49d394b0f7c494fb35b710c8c4520ab898783877abdd079013.png)

Which of the following is equivalent to ${\int }_{C}\,\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=2-4\operatorname{sin}⁡\theta \,$ for $\frac{\pi }{2}\le \theta \le \pi ,$ shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,

$$
{\int }_{C}\,\text{d}s=L.
$$

In polar coordinates, the arc length element $\text{d}s$ is given by

$$
\text{d}s=\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\frac{\text{d}r}{\text{d}\theta })}^{2}}\,\text{d}\theta .
$$

So, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\theta )$ for ${\theta }_{1}\le \theta \le {\theta }_{2},$ we have

$$
L={\int }_{C}\,\text{d}s={\int }_{{\theta }_{1}}^{{\theta }_{2}}\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\frac{\text{d}r}{\text{d}\theta })}^{2}}\,\text{d}\theta .
$$

Since $r=2-4\operatorname{sin}⁡\theta ,$ we have

$$
\frac{\text{d}r}{\text{d}\theta }=-4\operatorname{cos}⁡\theta .
$$

Therefore, the expression for our line integral is

$$
\begin{aligned}{\int }_{C}\,\text{d}s & ={\int }_{{\theta }_{1}}^{{\theta }_{2}}\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\frac{\text{d}r}{\text{d}\theta })}^{2}}\,\text{d}\theta  \\  & ={\int }_{\pi /2}^{\pi }\sqrt{(2-4\operatorname{sin}⁡\theta )^{2}+(-4\operatorname{cos}⁡\theta )^{2}}\,\text{d}\theta  \\  & ={\int }_{\pi /2}^{\pi }\sqrt{4-16\operatorname{sin}⁡\theta +16{sin}^{2}⁡\theta +16{cos}^{2}⁡\theta }\,\text{d}\theta  \\  & ={\int }_{\pi /2}^{\pi }\sqrt{4-16\operatorname{sin}⁡\theta +16}\,\text{d}\theta  \\  & ={\int }_{\pi /2}^{\pi }\sqrt{20-16\operatorname{sin}⁡\theta }\,\text{d}\theta  \\  & =2{\int }_{\pi /2}^{\pi }\sqrt{5-4\operatorname{sin}⁡\theta }\,\text{d}\theta .\end{aligned}
$$"} {:db/id 17592186259520 :question/id #uuid "77c73302-f62f-524d-b1a1-cbae03250f18" :question/math-academy-id "q-123395" :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186172089 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "973cf948-eeb3-5c46-b0ac-ee0307a8043a" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing Line Integrals of Unity Over Polar Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19e0accf-355f-56fb-b473-52acb69bd23b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\int }_{-\pi /6}^{7\pi /6}\sqrt{10+6\operatorname{cos}⁡4\theta +12{sin}^{2}⁡4\theta }\,\text{d}\theta " :db/id 17592186257002} {:answer/id #uuid "1a282cf9-e726-5893-9709-a1869d24dfa0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\int }_{-\pi /6}^{7\pi /6}\sqrt{17+8\operatorname{cos}⁡4\theta +15{sin}^{2}⁡4\theta }\,\text{d}\theta " :db/id 17592186257005} {:answer/id #uuid "58bdd781-32a3-5cea-bf8a-eda0cdb4360a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\int }_{-\pi /6}^{7\pi /6}\sqrt{12+6\operatorname{cos}⁡4\theta +10{sin}^{2}⁡4\theta }\,\text{d}\theta " :db/id 17592186257551} {:answer/id #uuid "7d2de95a-65cf-5b52-864b-9114a600f1ef" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\int }_{-\pi /6}^{7\pi /6}\sqrt{12+4\operatorname{cos}⁡4\theta +8{sin}^{2}⁡4\theta }\,\text{d}\theta " :db/id 17592186257887} {:answer/id #uuid "d1432cc5-bba9-5179-80ef-98471b08f92d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\int }_{-\pi /6}^{7\pi /6}\sqrt{4+8\operatorname{cos}⁡4\theta +12{sin}^{2}⁡4\theta }\,\text{d}\theta " :db/id 17592186258605}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a282cf9-e726-5893-9709-a1869d24dfa0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\int }_{-\pi /6}^{7\pi /6}\sqrt{17+8\operatorname{cos}⁡4\theta +15{sin}^{2}⁡4\theta }\,\text{d}\theta " :db/id 17592186257005} :answer-field/id #uuid "7adf0afe-c0b4-5de7-8baf-d650968a622b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186259239}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/problem "![](images/7d/7dc83cbf45ec3025ad33552ce889c7ecb2daf73b426bc2fd10ec932f3311c92a.png)

Which of the following is equivalent to ${\int }_{C}\,\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=4+\operatorname{cos}⁡4\theta \,$ for $-\frac{\pi }{6}\le \theta \le \frac{7\pi }{6},$ shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,

$$
{\int }_{C}\,\text{d}s=L.
$$

In polar coordinates, the arc length element $\text{d}s$ is given by

$$
\text{d}s=\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\frac{\text{d}r}{\text{d}\theta })}^{2}}\,\text{d}\theta .
$$

So, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\theta )$ for ${\theta }_{1}\le \theta \le {\theta }_{2},$ we have

$$
L={\int }_{C}\,\text{d}s={\int }_{{\theta }_{1}}^{{\theta }_{2}}\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\frac{\text{d}r}{\text{d}\theta })}^{2}}\,\text{d}\theta .
$$

Since $r=4+\operatorname{cos}⁡4\theta ,$ we have

$$
\frac{\text{d}r}{\text{d}\theta }=-4\operatorname{sin}⁡4\theta .
$$

Therefore, the expression for our line integral is

$$
\begin{aligned}{\int }_{C}\,\text{d}s & ={\int }_{{\theta }_{1}}^{{\theta }_{2}}\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\frac{\text{d}r}{\text{d}\theta })}^{2}}\,\text{d}\theta  \\  & ={\int }_{-\pi /6}^{7\pi /6}\sqrt{(4+\operatorname{cos}⁡4\theta )^{2}+(-4\operatorname{sin}⁡4\theta )^{2}}\,\text{d}\theta  \\  & ={\int }_{-\pi /6}^{7\pi /6}\sqrt{16+8\operatorname{cos}⁡4\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡4\theta +16{sin}^{2}⁡4\theta }\,\text{d}\theta  \\  & ={\int }_{-\pi /6}^{7\pi /6}\sqrt{16+8\operatorname{cos}⁡4\theta +1+15{sin}^{2}⁡4\theta }\,\text{d}\theta  \\  & ={\int }_{-\pi /6}^{7\pi /6}\sqrt{17+8\operatorname{cos}⁡4\theta +15{sin}^{2}⁡4\theta }\,\text{d}\theta \,.\end{aligned}
$$"} {:db/id 17592186363363 :question/id #uuid "929c6f43-55a6-50e3-98c8-0627bab8c6ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-149788"} {:db/id 17592186363370 :question/id #uuid "cd418bfc-f7c5-506e-bc72-5623879aa6a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-149798"} {:db/id 17592186400107 :question/id #uuid "ffdfd696-312e-58bb-aa35-858898cb0c00" :question/math-academy-id "q-123519"} {:db/id 17592186405029 :question/id #uuid "9debd71f-7b2d-5a32-ae10-ca946736e9d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-123458"} {:db/id 17592186447154 :question/id #uuid "586f6a61-ad62-56e5-9a2c-611ea0a2819c" :question/math-academy-id "q-123379"} {:db/id 17592186447157 :question/id #uuid "70661cc4-274a-5311-99fc-fa9cf5ff480d" :question/math-academy-id "q-123426"} {:db/id 17592186447158 :question/id #uuid "7aa4e4d5-0080-5cb6-bebe-9d6c94950127" :question/math-academy-id "q-123511"} {:db/id 17592186447159 :question/id #uuid "8c80223d-79b2-5d97-8d88-3fa6275a012a" :question/math-academy-id "q-123377"} {:db/id 17592186447160 :question/id #uuid "fb00dc0e-66fe-5382-b23d-00ac1fa661b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-123559"} {:db/id 17592186483352 :question/id #uuid "22b679e5-bd2f-5f85-b6e8-97dc0feb70a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-149799" :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d1c4079-2e4b-58ce-bcdd-cef5dccc4c25" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\int }_{0}^{\pi }{e}^{\theta }\sqrt{2{\theta }^{2}+2\theta +1}\,\text{d}\theta " :db/id 17592186482832} {:answer/id #uuid "2dfd78b7-9d7a-58b9-8cb4-d7bec505415d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\int }_{0}^{\pi }\sqrt{\theta {e}^{\theta }+1}\,\text{d}\theta " :db/id 17592186482890} {:answer/id #uuid "33feb924-d253-5720-9471-b530f5057780" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\int }_{0}^{\pi }{e}^{\theta }\sqrt{{\theta }^{2}+1}\,\text{d}\theta " :db/id 17592186482903} {:answer/id #uuid "4170ee29-2690-59a0-ac02-7ef811bc4abc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\int }_{0}^{\pi }\sqrt{{\theta }^{2}{e}^{2\theta }+1}\,\text{d}\theta " :db/id 17592186482922} {:answer/id #uuid "42a9d64f-13f1-500c-89f4-8b8e1616488f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\int }_{0}^{\pi }{e}^{2\theta }\sqrt{{\theta }^{2}+2}\,\text{d}\theta " :db/id 17592186482925}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id 17592186482832} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8b124e2-32da-5651-8541-39b8419c32ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493} :db/id 17592186483305}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487} :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\int }_{C}\,\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=\theta {e}^{\theta }\,$ for $0\le \theta \le \pi \,?$" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,

$$
{\int }_{C}\,\text{d}s=L.
$$

In polar coordinates, the arc length element $\text{d}s$ is given by

$$
\text{d}s=\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\frac{\text{d}r}{\text{d}\theta })}^{2}}\,\text{d}\theta .
$$

So, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\theta )$ for ${\theta }_{1}\le \theta \le {\theta }_{2},$ we have

$$
L={\int }_{C}\,\text{d}s={\int }_{{\theta }_{1}}^{{\theta }_{2}}\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\frac{\text{d}r}{\text{d}\theta })}^{2}}\,\text{d}\theta .
$$

Since $r=\theta {e}^{\theta },$ we have

$$
\frac{\text{d}r}{\text{d}\theta }=(1+\theta ){e}^{\theta }.
$$

Therefore, the expression for our line integral is

$$
\begin{aligned}{\int }_{C}\,\text{d}s & ={\int }_{{\theta }_{1}}^{{\theta }_{2}}\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\frac{\text{d}r}{\text{d}\theta })}^{2}}\,\text{d}\theta  \\  & ={\int }_{0}^{\pi }\sqrt{{(\theta {e}^{\theta })}^{2}+{((1+\theta ){e}^{\theta })}^{2}}\,\text{d}\theta  \\  & ={\int }_{0}^{\pi }\sqrt{{\theta }^{2}{e}^{2\theta }+(1+\theta )^{2}{e}^{2\theta }}\,\text{d}\theta  \\  & ={\int }_{0}^{\pi }\sqrt{{e}^{2\theta }({\theta }^{2}+1+2\theta +{\theta }^{2})}\,\text{d}\theta  \\  & ={\int }_{0}^{\pi }{e}^{\theta }\sqrt{2{\theta }^{2}+2\theta +1}\,\text{d}\theta .\end{aligned}
$$"} {:db/id 17592186483367 :question/id #uuid "49e7f17d-b251-56fb-a73b-9aa663a5feb5" :question/math-academy-id "q-123410" :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07e65c36-be44-5f01-9c76-e2d474ae0429" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\int }_{\pi /6}^{7\pi /6}\sqrt{16+10\operatorname{cos}⁡\theta }\,\text{d}\theta " :db/id 17592186482823} {:answer/id #uuid "416c60e9-e558-531b-9580-88325c39f159" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\int }_{\pi /6}^{7\pi /6}\sqrt{18-8\operatorname{cos}⁡\theta }\,\text{d}\theta " :db/id 17592186482921} {:answer/id #uuid "d1393249-3bf9-5edc-8048-09a57dbf1249" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\int }_{\pi /6}^{7\pi /6}\sqrt{8-18\operatorname{cos}⁡\theta }\,\text{d}\theta " :db/id 17592186483160} {:answer/id #uuid "d1fcdafd-bf7d-5de0-8c88-50a987f3891f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\int }_{\pi /6}^{7\pi /6}\sqrt{26-10\operatorname{cos}⁡\theta }\,\text{d}\theta " :db/id 17592186483163} {:answer/id #uuid "e65466c4-bccb-5f49-930c-7f3a5aefd1ef" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\int }_{\pi /6}^{7\pi /6}\sqrt{10-26\operatorname{cos}⁡\theta }\,\text{d}\theta " :db/id 17592186483201}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id 17592186483163} :answer-field/id #uuid "86b07591-e098-5b5d-80e3-d30542d3cd68" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493} :db/id 17592186483290}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485} :question/problem "![](images/ef/ef5fe44009ea42457243052cff584a216ee94636d8e3c16ff9446e85b8eca551.png)

Which of the following is equivalent to ${\int }_{C}\,\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=1-5\operatorname{cos}⁡\theta \,$ for $\frac{\pi }{6}\le \theta \le \frac{7\pi }{6},$ shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,

$$
{\int }_{C}\,\text{d}s=L.
$$

In polar coordinates, the arc length element $\text{d}s$ is given by

$$
\text{d}s=\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\frac{\text{d}r}{\text{d}\theta })}^{2}}\,\text{d}\theta .
$$

So, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\theta )$ for ${\theta }_{1}\le \theta \le {\theta }_{2},$ we have

$$
L={\int }_{C}\,\text{d}s={\int }_{{\theta }_{1}}^{{\theta }_{2}}\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\frac{\text{d}r}{\text{d}\theta })}^{2}}\,\text{d}\theta .
$$

Since $r=1-5\operatorname{cos}⁡\theta ,$ we have 

$$
\frac{\text{d}r}{\text{d}\theta }=5\operatorname{sin}⁡\theta .
$$

Therefore, the expression for our line integral is

$$
\begin{aligned}{\int }_{C}\,\text{d}s & ={\int }_{{\theta }_{1}}^{{\theta }_{2}}\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\frac{\text{d}r}{\text{d}\theta })}^{2}}\,\text{d}\theta  \\  & ={\int }_{\pi /6}^{7\pi /6}\sqrt{(1-5\operatorname{cos}⁡\theta )^{2}+(5\operatorname{sin}⁡\theta )^{2}}\,\text{d}\theta  \\  & ={\int }_{\pi /6}^{7\pi /6}\sqrt{1-10\operatorname{cos}⁡\theta +25{cos}^{2}⁡\theta +25{sin}^{2}⁡\theta }\,\text{d}\theta  \\  & ={\int }_{\pi /6}^{7\pi /6}\sqrt{1-10\operatorname{cos}⁡\theta +25}\,\text{d}\theta  \\  & ={\int }_{\pi /6}^{7\pi /6}\sqrt{26-10\operatorname{cos}⁡\theta }\,\text{d}\theta \,.\end{aligned}
