Geometrically, we can think of $\textrm ds$ as representing the change in arc length that arises due to a vanishingly small change in $t.$ By summing (integrating) all of these small changes, we arrive at the arc length of $C.$ We'll explore this in more detail at the end of the lesson." :tutorial/id #uuid "56e8bdd3-5303-5eeb-82d3-ef837773c38f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5797 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "65361afd-5d8b-59a0-8bed-041764bc0685" :step/math-academy-id 35544 :step/next {:db/id 17592186172996}} {:db/id 17592186172993 :step/content {:db/id 17592186172089 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186171895 :question/id #uuid "f6f11512-c37f-5922-b642-5290003b1d91" :question/math-academy-id "e-9492"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "973cf948-eeb3-5c46-b0ac-ee0307a8043a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id 17592186049212} {:db/id 17592186050779}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186172288} {:db/id 17592186172289} {:db/id 17592186259519} {:db/id 17592186259520} {:db/id 17592186363363} {:db/id 17592186363370} {:db/id 17592186400107} {:db/id 17592186405029} {:db/id 17592186447154} {:db/id 17592186447157} {:db/id 17592186447158} {:db/id 17592186447159} {:db/id 17592186447160} {:db/id 17592186483352} {:db/id 17592186483367} {:db/id 17592186483421}] :knowledge-point/title "Constructing Line Integrals of Unity Over Polar Curves"} :step/id #uuid "ba2fdaeb-e9c0-578e-a23e-d08f48959fe8" :step/math-academy-id 35542 :step/next {:db/id 17592186172994}} {:db/id 17592186172994 :step/content {:db/id 17592186172090 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186171896 :question/id #uuid "6753365e-9915-5be9-9a6d-d4c40b2caea0" :question/math-academy-id "e-9493"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5777905c-e581-5454-bdbe-29878ad1237b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id 17592186048717} {:db/id 17592186049303}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186172287} {:db/id 17592186172290} {:db/id 17592186259536} {:db/id 17592186275248} {:db/id 1759