" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\int }_{0}^{\pi }\sqrt{2\operatorname{cos}⁡\theta +2}\,\text{d}\theta " :db/id 17592186483061} {:answer/id #uuid "acc1c101-f384-5d1e-ab31-e736b726cbf0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\int }_{0}^{\pi }\sqrt{\operatorname{cos}⁡2\theta +1}\,\text{d}\theta " :db/id 17592186483104} {:answer/id #uuid "b1c5c96c-0c63-5cb2-88af-e9d02ac33333" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\int }_{0}^{\pi }\sqrt{2{cos}^{2}⁡\theta +2}\,\text{d}\theta " :db/id 17592186483115}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id 17592186483061} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf074d1f-8612-5220-b189-30860f156e17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493} :db/id 17592186483319}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e4160743-42ed-5b1d-80ee-6f99ed405134" :question/math-academy-id "q-149786" :question/problem "![](images/24/24f7ae31455abc10e60814b6b599966c92680422432c82f6cc7386d6d414f29c.png)

Which of the following is equivalent to ${\int }_{C}\,\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=\operatorname{cos}⁡\theta +1$ for $0\le \theta \le \pi ,$ shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,

$$
{\int }_{C}\,\text{d}s=L.
$$

In polar coordinates, the arc length element $\text{d}s$ is given by

$$
\text{d}s=\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\frac{\text{d}r}{\text{d}\theta })}^{2}}\,\text{d}\theta .
$$

So, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\theta )$ for ${\theta }_{1}\le \theta \le {\theta }_{2},$ we have

$$
L={\int }_{C}\,\text{d}s={\int }_{{\theta }_{1}}^{{\theta }_{2}}\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\frac{\text{d}r}{\text{d}\theta })}^{2}}\,\text{d}\theta .
$$

Since $r=\operatorname{cos}⁡\theta +1,$ we have

$$
\frac{\text{d}r}{\text{d}\theta }=-\operatorname{sin}⁡\theta .
$$

Therefore, the expression for our line integral is

$$
