Given that the eigenvalues of $\mathbf{f}''(\mathbf{a})$ are $\lambda_1 = -1$ and $\lambda_2 = -3,$ what type of point is $\mathbf{a}?$" :question/worked-solution "The point $\mathbf{a} \in \mathbb{R}^n$ is a critical point of the function $f : \mathbb{R}^n \to \mathbb{R}$ if $\mathbf{f}'(\mathbf{a}) = \mathbf{0}^T.$ In such cases, we can classify $\mathbf{a}$ as follows:

- if $\mathbf{f}''(\mathbf{a})$ is positive definite, then $\mathbf{a}$ is a local minimum

- if $\mathbf{f}''(\mathbf{a})$ is negative definite, then $\mathbf{a}$ is a local maximum

- if $\mathbf{f}''(\mathbf{a})$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $\mathbf{a}$ is a saddle point

The quadratic Taylor expansion of a function $f : \mathbb{R}^n \to \mathbb{R}$ about $\mathbf{x} = \mathbf{a} \in \mathbb{R}^n$ given by

$$
f(\mathbf{x}) \approx f(\mathbf{a}) + \mathbf{f}'(\mathbf{a})(\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{a})
+
\dfrac12(\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{a})^T
\boldsymbol{f}''(\mathbf{a})
(\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{a}).
$$

Since the quadratic Taylor expansion of $f$ has no $(\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{a})$ term, we can conclude that $\boldsymbol{f}'(\mathbf{a}) = \mathbf{0}^T.$ Hence, $\mathbf a$ is a critical point of $f.$

Finally, notice that both the eigenvalues of $\boldsymbol{f}''(\mathbf{a})$ are negative:

$$
\begin{aligned}{\lambda }_{1} & =-1<0 \\ {\lambda }_{2} & =-3<0\end{aligned}
$$

So, the matrix is negative definite. Therefore, $\mathbf{a}$ is a local maximum of $f.$"} {:db/id 17592186048555} {:db/id 17592186124820 :question/id #uuid "e025078b-b821-5afd-8d42-a7c7c420b244" :question/math-academy-id "q-163721" :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124648 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "233eab4c-ab17-53a8-ae03-f6f28ac78922" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Local Extrema Using Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82b674c8-eb57-5887-8426-8d408fa1479c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inconclusive" :db/id 17592186121518} {:answer/id #uuid "eb2a4026-9a30-57e8-a31c-3c06788c36b0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "not a critical point" :db/id 17592186121519} {:answer/id #uuid "207f2967-8496-5a9e-88f8-aba4ca44f2e9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "saddle point" :db/id 17592186121520} {:answer/id #uuid "528656e1-de5b-5574-9c6d-9c343b8f9305" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "local maximum" :db/id 17592186121521} {:answer/id #uuid "58e91dd6-8a5c-5dac-bf79-fcf5d7a52091" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "local minimum" :db/id 17592186121522}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58e91dd6-8a5c-5dac-bf79-fcf5d7a52091" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "local minimum" :db/id 17592186121522} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2522a01-cce7-51e3-a966-267ce3351d43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186121517}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\in {R}^{2}$ is given by

