Given that the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are ${\lambda }_{1}=3-\sqrt{5}$ and ${\lambda }_{2}=3+\sqrt{5},$ what type of point is $a?$" :question/worked-solution "The point $a\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:

- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum

- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum

- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point

The quadratic Taylor expansion of a function $f:{R}^{n}\to R$ about $x=a\in {R}^{n}$ given by

$$
f(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a).
$$

Since the quadratic Taylor expansion of $f$ has no $(x-a)$ term, we can conclude that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ Hence, $a$ is a critical point of $f.$

Finally, notice that both the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are positive:

$$
\begin{aligned}{\lambda }_{1} & =3-\sqrt{5}>0 \\ {\lambda }_{2} & =3+\sqrt{5}>0\end{aligned}
$$

So, the matrix is positive definite. Therefore, $a$ is a local minimum of $f.$"} {:db/id 17592186124823 :question/id #uuid "a10a1d5a-8a2c-54e0-8fb3-61f7b8f41913" :question/math-academy-id "q-163787"} {:db/id 17592186284417 :question/id #uuid "d9c2261a-709f-5777-b889-b197f8b76cee" :question/math-academy-id "q-163773"} {:db/id 17592186284420 :question/id #uuid "b4393f82-6adc-53c8-a484-4676593db469" :question/math-academy-id "q-163782"} {:db/id 17592186284421 :question/id #uuid "c58156b9-c2e5-56d6-934d-438ab8169ee9" :question/math-academy-id "q-163784" :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124648 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "233eab4c-ab17-53a8-ae03-f6f28ac78922" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Local Extrema Using Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ea10e1f-ce81-5b81-a050-f67d321d8e92" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "local maximum" :db/id 17592186281817} {:answer/id #uuid "23775611-5ec1-536e-a368-f7631073efb7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "saddle point" :db/id 17592186282003} {:answer/id #uuid "34a1b032-0917-5bc2-9368-443a9efff873" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "local minimum" :db/id 17592186282157} {:answer/id #uuid "7b0ae5cd-372a-5f03-ba28-d378c3c5fd4e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inconclusive" :db/id 17592186282745} {:answer/id #uuid "955fea63-7ba6-5578-af41-a7a1228d5dc6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "not a critical point" :db/id 17592186282978}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ea10e1f-ce81-5b81-a050-f67d321d8e92" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "local maximum" :db/id 17592186281817} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7c9e8aa-d1c7-5a2a-96d1-342a8e10e7d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186284288}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\in {R}^{3}$ is given by

$$
