 :answer/value "-\sin te^{\cos t + \sin t}" :db/id 17592186132306} {:activity/expected-seconds 840.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id 17592186134864} :activity/id #uuid "80a63c78-ab52-5147-b1df-b7736290c5a2" :activity/scope {:db/id 17592186050567 :topic/id #uuid "54747772-1cac-572e-9b8f-be3a6c8abe45" :topic/math-academy-id 2198} :activity/steps [{:db/id 17592186134864 :step/content {:db/id 17592186135131 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $\mathbf F =P\,\mathbf i + Q\,\mathbf j$ is a vector field on $\mathbb R^2,$ and a region $D$ is bounded by a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve $C,$ as shown below. 

![](images/fa/fa3efbc9200a6acdda01e503999f7588cd44fd63af77fa0a6abf1b5303c9a889.png)

Green's theorem states that

$$
\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\frac{∂P}{∂y})\,\text{d}A={∮}_{C}P\,\text{d}x+Q\,\text{d}y.\end{aligned}
$$



Let's now consider what happens when the scalar curl term on the left-hand side of the above equation equals unity everywhere on $D.$ In other words, let's consider what happens when

$$
\dfrac{\partial Q}{\partial x} - \dfrac{\partial P}{\partial y}=1.
$$

In this case, the double integral gives the area of $D,$ because

$$
\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\frac{∂P}{∂y})\,\text{d}A={∬}_{D}\,\text{d}x\text{d}y=\text{Area}(D).\end{aligned}
$$



Thus, we've just discovered a new method of computing the area of $D.$ We simply choose a vector field $\mathbf{F}(x,y)$ whose scalar curl equals unity everywhere on $D,$ and then compute its circulation over $C.$

There are infinitely many vector fields that we can use to compute the area of a region. Some of the most common for this purpose are as follows:

$$
\mathbf{F}(x,y)= \left\langle -y, \: 0 \right\rangle, 
\qquad
\mathbf{G}(x,y)= \left\langle -\dfrac{y}{2}, \: \dfrac{x}{2} \right\rangle,
\qquad
\mathbf{H}(x,y)= \left\langle 0, \: x \right\rangle
$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "bbaebc78-d8b2-5500-9c52-f82bd3d3a8a5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5839 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "25733548-0d69-5314-abb7-7cf1ba40a7bc" :step/math-academy-id 35784 :step/next {:db/id 17592186134868}} {:db/id 17592186134865 :step/content {:db/id 17592186133895 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186133711 :question/id #uuid "a817c3c6-97bb-55e4-9a1e-04ed99d3eaab" :question/math-academy-id "e-9553"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f4f726e-d985-5915-a4d0-30cfa6f4b89b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id 17592186051070}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186134092} {:db/id 17592186134093} {:db/id 17592186369359} {:db/id 17592186369366} {:db/id 17592186369373} {:db/id 17592186431048} {:db/id 17592186431050}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Fields That Satisfy the Unit Curl Property"} :step/id #uuid "31e81ee5-877c-5590-999c-e1a635893093" :step/math-academy-id 35785 :step/next {:db/id 17592186134866}} {:db/id 17592186134866 :step/content {:db/id 17592186133896 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186133712 :question/id #uuid "74c9ecfb-5005-53ff-b7a9-db798488587b" :question/math-academy-id "e-9554"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8644454b-ee25-59fd-ae65-9770a0b44442" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id 17592186050666}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186134377} {:db/id 17592186134378} {:db/id 17592186369413} {:db/id 17592186369420} {:db/id 17592186370913} {:db/id 17592186405050} {:db/id 17592186431049} {:db/id 17592186431053} {:db/id 17592186442249} {:db/id 17592186442250} {:db/id 17592186442252}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Area Bounded by a Closed Curve Using Green’s Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "3ff52bb4-279b-55a3-9202-9e4939a84e2e" :step/math-academy-id 35786 :step/next {:db/id 17592186134867}} {:db/id 17592186134867 :step/content {:db/id 17592186133897 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186133713 :question/id #uuid "aa4400ff-09b0-596c-9857-c13881b72f73" :question/math-academy-id "e-9555"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf9b73fa-c7ce-5c93-9131-ec36d29d6c3a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186134376} {:db/id 17592186134386} {:db/id 17592186369399} {:db/id 17592186369406} {:db/id 17592186369427} {:db/id 17592186369448} {:db/id 17592186431051} {:db/id 17592186431052} {:db/id 17592186442251}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Area Using Green's Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "0aca8ded-b498-5e7b-a4b3-06277e60cbf3" :step/math-academy-id 35787} {:db/id 17592186134868 :step/content {:db/id 17592186135132 :tutorial/content "As a concrete example, let $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C,$ parametrized as

$$
\mathbf{r}(t) = \langle 2\cos t, \: \sin t \rangle, \qquad t \in \left [0, 2\pi \right).
$$

The region $D$ and its positively oriented bounding curve $C$ are shown below.

![](images/a6/a6d9fe9cad4ccee56d5d61457c475b41371e0705f1aeccd3a18fb4a3fa8e127c.png)

To calculate the area of $D$ using Green's theorem, we need to find a vector field whose scalar curl equals unity everywhere on $D.$ So, let's choose

$$
P(x,y) = -y, \qquad Q(x,y) = 0.
$$

Indeed, we have

$$
\dfrac{\partial Q}{\partial x} - \dfrac{\partial P}{\partial y} = 0 - \left(-1\right) = 1. \qquad{\color{green}{\checkmark}}
$$

Therefore, since $C$ is a positively oriented path, the area of $D$ is given by

$$
\begin{aligned}\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\text{d}A \\  & ={∬}_{D}(\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\frac{∂P}{∂y})\,\text{d}A \\  & ={∮}_{C}P\,\text{d}x+Q\,\text{d}y.\end{aligned}
$$



Substituting our expressions for $P$ and $Q$ into the above, we get

$$
\oint\limits_C P \: \text{d}x + Q \: \text{d}y = -\oint\limits_C y \: \text{d}x.
$$



Let's now evaluate the integral using the usual methods. The derivative of $\mathbf{r}(t)$ is

$$
\mathbf{r'}(t) = \langle -2\sin t, \: \cos t \rangle.
$$

So we can calculate our line integral as follows:

$$
\begin{aligned}-{∮}_{C}y\,\text{d}x & =-{\int }_{0}^{2\pi }\operatorname{sin}⁡t\cdot (-2\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\,dt \\  & ={\int }_{0}^{2\pi }2{sin}^{2}⁡t\,dt \\  & ={\int }_{0}^{2\pi }2(\frac{1-\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{2})\,dt \\  & ={\int }_{0}^{2\pi }1-\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\,dt \\  & ={[t-\frac{1}{2}\operatorname{sin}⁡2t]}_{0}^{2\pi } \\  & =2\pi \end{aligned}
$$

Therefore, we conclude that

$$
\textrm{Area}(D)  =2\pi.
$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f13baac9-fbd8-5a28-b168-f3010a604b1b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5840 :tutorial/title "A Concrete Example"} :step/id #uuid "f6584994-83e5-5672-aa98-e6e1491a031c" :step/math-academy-id 35792 :step/next {:db/id 17592186134876}} {:db/id 17592186134876 :step/content {:db/id 17592186135137 :tutorial/content "We've established that if a region $D$ is bounded by a positively oriented curve $C,$ and we select a vector field $\mathbf F = P\,\mathbf i + Q\,\mathbf j$ such that

