Let $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as
$r(t)=⟨\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\,\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩$ for $t\in [\frac{\pi }{3},\frac{5\pi }{3}].$ The area of $D$ can be written as

$$
\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA={\int }_{\pi /3}^{5\pi /3}\boxed{}\,dt,
$$

where $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$ Determine the missing part of the equation." :question/worked-solution "Our simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented, and  

$$
\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.
$$

So, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:

$$
\begin{aligned}\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\text{d}A \\  & ={∬}_{D}(\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\frac{∂P}{∂y})\text{d}A \\  & ={∮}_{C}P\,\text{d}x+Q\,\text{d}y\end{aligned}
$$



Substituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get

$$
{∮}_{C}P\,\text{d}x+Q\,\text{d}y={∮}_{C}-y\,\text{d}x.
$$



The derivative of $r(t)$ is

$$
{r}^{′}(t)=⟨-\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\,\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩.
$$

Therefore, we can write our line integral as

$$
\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}-y\,dx & ={\int }_{\pi /3}^{5\pi /3}-(\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)\cdot (-\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)\,dt \\  & ={\int }_{\pi /3}^{5\pi /3}(\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)(\operatorname{sin}⁡t-2\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)\,dt.\end{aligned}
$$"} :step/id #uuid "26451fe3-7951-51a8-9a1b-fd3d8660fd3b" :step/next {:db/id 17592186442256}} {:db/id 17592186442254 :step/content {:db/id 17592186134386 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86f48c0b-2a3d-5163-b8e8-0c7233eb05fb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\frac{8}{5}" :db/id 17592186132356} {:answer/id #uuid "e01d132b-6218-5a49-8b31-e23dbbbfbe93" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\frac{7}{3}" :db/id 17592186132357} {:answer/id #uuid "ed54db10-308f-5357-92ca-c739526b9c08" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\frac{4}{3}" :db/id 17592186132358} {:answer/id #uuid "fab5eb83-c4a0-5d86-82d7-ebea1c4f60b9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id 17592186132359} {:answer/id #uuid "6d37aff8-08ac-5983-b3ea-04ddf202a770" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id 17592186132360}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id 17592186132356} :answer-field/id #uuid "30db4754-f10f-5c4e-a207-166a2bda9bd6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493} :db/id 17592186132355}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c299dba6-b9c4-559b-b05a-803d3465ef0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-42298" :question/problem "![](images/01/01a5f06674cacca4764f438a8c06f020659f0177a0b74952f40bc9072627ec3f.png)

Consider the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C={C}_{1}\cup {C}_{2},$ where 

- ${C}_{1}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{1}(t)=2{t}^{2}\,i-{t}^{3}\,j$ for $t\in [-1,1],$ and

- ${C}_{2}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{2}(t)=2\,i+t\,j$ for $t\in [-1,1].$

Use Green's theorem to find the area of the region." :question/worked-solution "First, let $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$

Our simple, piecewise-smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented and  

$$
\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.
$$

So, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:

$$
\begin{aligned}\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\text{d}A \\  & ={∬}_{D}(\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\frac{∂P}{∂y})\text{d}A \\  & ={∮}_{C}P\,\text{d}x+Q\,\text{d}y\end{aligned}
$$



Substituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get

$$
{∮}_{C}P\,\text{d}x+Q\,\text{d}y=-{∮}_{C}y\,\text{d}x.
$$



The derivatives of ${r}_{1}(t)$ and ${r}_{2}(t)$ are as follows:

$$
\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:\, & {r}_{1}^{′}(t)=4t\,i-3{t}^{2}\,j \\ {C}_{2}:\, & {r}_{2}^{′}(t)=j\end{aligned}
$$

Therefore, we can write our line integral as

$$
\begin{aligned}-{∮}_{C}y\,\text{d}x & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}\cup {C}_{2}}y\,dx \\  & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}}y\,dx-{∮}_{{C}_{2}}y\,dx \\  & =-{\int }_{-1}^{1}(-{t}^{3})\cdot 4t\,\text{d}t-{\int }_{-1}^{1}t\cdot 0\,\text{d}t \\  & =4{\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{4}\,\text{d}t-0 \\  & =4[\frac{{t}^{5}}{5}]_{-1}^{1} \\  & =4\cdot \frac{2}{5} \\  & =\frac{8}{5}.\end{aligned}
$$"} :step/id #uuid "3efe2d38-47eb-5856-81a2-431378bd1194" :step/next {:db/id 17592186442253}} {:db/id 17592186442255 :step/content {:db/id 17592186442251 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "079819d9-6be2-54b5-a40a-e93e9bbceca5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\frac{12}{5}" :db/id 17592186442226} {:answer/id #uuid "30812918-b09d-5eac-98bf-46dd91f25063" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id 17592186442229} {:answer/id #uuid "6baabb12-e16e-5f6d-acae-67851198dc97" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id 17592186442234} {:answer/id #uuid "9beda9f3-8b47-5843-a2c9-b0565509b47e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "7\pi " :db/id 17592186442237} {:answer/id #uuid "a6e358f5-54ad-55b9-afe1-8f1e0f017b3c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id 17592186442238}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id 17592186442238} :answer-field/id #uuid "589fe02c-92ec-567f-8354-84271d506420" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493} :db/id 17592186442247}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "55130439-a2e5-560a-bbed-4ce47334253d" :question/math-academy-id "q-126757" :question/problem "![](images/ef/ef44f01fb462cdf608f66a60dd11b8df798603bd204dca9d579ed50b370a84e3.png)

Use Green's theorem to find the area of the region enclosed by the curve 
$C$ parametrized as $r(t)=-3\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\,i-\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\,j$ 
for $t\in [0,\frac{\pi }{2}].$" :question/worked-solution "First, let $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$

Our simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and  

$$
\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.
$$

So, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:

$$
\begin{aligned}\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\text{d}A \\  & ={∬}_{D}(\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\frac{∂P}{∂y})\text{d}A \\  & =-{∮}_{C}P\,\text{d}x+Q\,\text{d}y\end{aligned}
$$



Notice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since our path is negatively oriented. 

Substituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get

$$
-{∮}_{C}P\,\text{d}x+Q\,\text{d}y={∮}_{C}y\,\text{d}x.
$$



The derivative of $r(t)$ is

$$
\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =-6\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\,i-4\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\,j.\end{aligned}
$$

Finally, we can evaluate our line integral using the substitution $u=\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\text{d}u=-2\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\,\text{d}t,$ as follows:

$$
\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}y\,dx & ={\int }_{0}^{\pi /2}-\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\cdot (-6\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)\,\text{d}t \\  & =6{\int }_{0}^{\pi /2}\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\,\text{d}t \\  & =6{\int }_{0}^{\pi /2}2\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\cdot \operatorname{cos}⁡2t\,\text{d}t \\  & =-6{\int }_{0}^{\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡2t\cdot (-2\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)\,\text{d}t \\  & =-6{\int }_{1}^{-1}{u}^{2}\,\text{d}u \\  & =6{\int }_{-1}^{1}{u}^{2}\,\text{d}u \\  & =2{u}^{3}{|}_{-1}^{1} \\  & =2({1}^{3}-(-1)^{3}) \\  & =4\end{aligned}
$$"} :step/id #uuid "7f777294-56ab-5734-acea-b53e1763dfff"} {:db/id 17592186442256 :step/content {:db/id 17592186442250 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01f00612-ca24-58a9-9a99-5bc9dff70695" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{sin}^{3}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t" :db/id 17592186442225} {:answer/id #uuid "244e3377-d03b-59bc-9a15-821691749cc3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{sin}^{4}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t" :db/id 17592186442228} {:answer/id #uuid "3b19e5e7-17b2-5ecd-8bda-2f5d049ec56f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{4}⁡t" :db/id 17592186442230} {:answer/id #uuid "4ac2fdfe-d325-50d4-8f82-69274f9797ee" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{sin}^{3}⁡t{cos}^{4}⁡t" :db/id 17592186442231} {:answer/id #uuid "4c93679f-96a9-5546-b831-28f2a59ed0bd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{sin}^{3}⁡t{cos}^{3}⁡t" :db/id 17592186442232}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id 17592186442228} :answer-field/id #uuid "1007d99f-4a44-5eca-8672-e3953d3b25aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493} :db/id 17592186442245}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "30739161-7e72-5e37-b131-9975b77440f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-42315" :question/problem "![](images/bf/bfec7cbdb26a3db773ab1f6207de436ff0856b331cda183da530dbb6b91d91bd.png)

