</table>" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then

- $Σ$ is a $3\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,

- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\times 3$ matrix,

- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\times 2$ matrix.

From the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\sigma }_{1}=2\sqrt{5}$ and ${\sigma }_{2}=0.$
Also, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$

Therefore, we can compute the first column of $U$ as follows:

$$
\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\frac{1}{{\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\  & =\frac{1}{2\sqrt{5}}\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\ 1 & 1 \\ 3 & 3\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} \\ \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\end{bmatrix} \\  & =\frac{1}{2\sqrt{5}}\begin{bmatrix}0 \\ \frac{2}{\sqrt{2}} \\ \frac{6}{\sqrt{2}}\end{bmatrix} \\  & =\begin{bmatrix}0 \\ \frac{1}{\sqrt{10}} \\ \frac{3}{\sqrt{10}}\end{bmatrix}\end{aligned}
$$

Since the second singular value is ${\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find vectors 

$$
u=\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\ {x}_{2} \\ {x}_{3}\end{bmatrix}
$$

such that ${u}_{1}\cdot u=0$ and $‖u‖=1.$

Solving ${u}_{1}\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to

$$
\frac{1}{\sqrt{10}}{x}_{2}+\frac{3}{\sqrt{10}}{x}_{3}=0\,⟹\,{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=0,
$$

we get the general solution 

$$
\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\ -3{x}_{3} \\ {x}_{3}\end{bmatrix}={x}_{1}{{\begin{bmatrix}1 \\ 0 \\ 0\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{x}_{3}{{\begin{bmatrix}0 \\ -3 \\ 1\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}},
$$

where ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are free variables.

Notice that the two linearly independent solutions ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ are mutually orthogonal. Therefore, we only need to normalize them. This gives the second and third columns of $U:$   

$$
{u}_{2}=\begin{bmatrix}1 \\ 0 \\ 0\end{bmatrix}\,{u}_{3}=\begin{bmatrix}0 \\ -\frac{3}{\sqrt{10}} \\ \frac{1}{\sqrt{10}}\end{bmatrix}
$$

So, the matrix $U$ could be the following:

$$
U=\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\ \frac{1}{\sqrt{10}} & 0 & -\frac{3}{\sqrt{10}} \\ \frac{3}{\sqrt{10}} & 0 & \frac{1}{\sqrt{10}}\end{bmatrix}
$$"} {:db/id 17592186162043 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186161837 :question/id #uuid "4fd6d412-d080-5fd1-9318-65a0f5f02dc6" :question/math-academy-id "e-8634"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8b36c482-75a0-5413-8e3e-4a72e3ddb26a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id 17592186049951}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186162712 :question/id #uuid "0acab5c5-194e-580b-922b-276032b93c12" :question/math-academy-id "q-97365"} {:db/id 17592186162725 :question/id #uuid "dcd0e8b8-71e5-5dcd-a370-0847d15e9434" :question/math-academy-id "q-98797"} {:db/id 17592186371422 :question/id #uuid "fd52405b-2747-5b7b-9ac8-728557a18405" :question/math-academy-id "q-98769"} {:db/id 17592186371429 :question/id #uuid "5dfe9dfb-2de9-5049-bb36-b64e2c2a8946" :question/math-academy-id "q-98770"} {:db/id 17592186371436 :question/id #uuid "1fbce2d1-be2d-5c6a-bfaf-0486d4c0daa8" :question/math-academy-id "q-98772"} {:db/id 17592186399322 :question/id #uuid "ea8f7f21-702e-5433-ab10-60c9a800aee5" :question/math-academy-id "q-98765"} {:db/id 17592186399329 :question/id #uuid "4670b2c4-578c-543d-913f-4a2e02949175" :question/math-academy-id "q-98768"} {:db/id 17592186408020 :question/id #uuid "256724b0-7532-517f-89d9-acc3daa6cf56" :question/math-academy-id "q-98766"} {:db/id 17592186423859 :question/id #uuid "7cbede22-0844-5470-8655-1901a08d01cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-140963"} {:db/id 17592186423864 :question/id #uuid "bfd2aceb-4fd0-5447-93c2-1fcb15c8449b" :question/math-academy-id "q-98767"} {:db/id 17592186443406 :question/id #uuid "77278df4-e113-5900-8941-27a77a33d7c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-98696"} {:db/id 17592186497345 :question/id #uuid "2c32a996-4944-5e2d-afe2-519907ae493e" :question/math-academy-id "q-140962"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Factor in the SVD of a Rectangular Matrix"} {:db/id 17592186161837 :question/id #uuid "4fd6d412-d080-5fd1-9318-65a0f5f02dc6" :question/math-academy-id "e-8634"} {:db/id 17592186162712 :question/id #uuid "0acab5c5-194e-580b-922b-276032b93c12" :question/math-academy-id "q-97365" :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46ec70b2-790d-582b-8940-37eea292ae4f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\begin{bmatrix}\frac{2}{\sqrt{5}} \\ \frac{1}{\sqrt{5}} \\ 0\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186430279} {:answer/id #uuid "4d30fcb9-4e9c-5480-91f8-be9027ba4304" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\begin{bmatrix}\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} \\ \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} \\ 0\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186430281} {:answer/id #uuid "5ea213a0-7cb0-59f5-98bd-30dcad60aa90" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\begin{bmatrix}0 \\ 0 \\ 1\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186430287} {:answer/id #uuid "84575063-53ca-5b2a-94b3-9e965df7c601" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\begin{bmatrix}\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} \\ 0 \\ -\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186430299} {:answer/id #uuid "d346a1ab-d6ee-5497-ae67-93ca1862ead9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\begin{bmatrix}1 \\ 0 \\ 0\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186430325}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id 17592186430325} :answer-field/id #uuid "53075006-3bde-5335-a07e-7b2f11fef0a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493} :db/id 17592186161564}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487} :question/problem "$$
A=\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\ 2 & 2 \\ 1 & -2\end{bmatrix},\,Σ=\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\ 0 & 2 \\ 0 & 0\end{bmatrix},\,V=\begin{bmatrix}\frac{1}{\sqrt{5}} & -\frac{2}{\sqrt{5}} \\ \frac{2}{\sqrt{5}} & \frac{1}{\sqrt{5}}\end{bmatrix}
$$

Consider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the $3$rd column of the matrix $U?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then

- $Σ$ is a $3\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,

- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\times 3$ matrix,

- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\times 2$ matrix.

From the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\sigma }_{1}=3$ and ${\sigma }_{2}=2.$
Also, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$

Therefore, we can compute the $1$st and $2$nd columns of $U$ as follows:

- First, we compute ${u}_{1}:$

$$
\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\frac{1}{{\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\  & =\frac{1}{3}\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\ 2 & 2 \\ 1 & -2\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}\frac{1}{\sqrt{5}} \\ \frac{2}{\sqrt{5}}\end{bmatrix} \\  & =\frac{1}{3}\begin{bmatrix}0 \\ \frac{6}{\sqrt{5}} \\ -\frac{3}{\sqrt{5}}\end{bmatrix} \\  & =\begin{bmatrix}0 \\ \frac{2}{\sqrt{5}} \\ -\frac{1}{\sqrt{5}}\end{bmatrix}\end{aligned}
$$

- Then, we compute ${u}_{2}:$

$$
\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\frac{1}{{\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\  & =\frac{1}{2}\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\ 2 & 2 \\ 1 & -2\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}-\frac{2}{\sqrt{5}} \\ \frac{1}{\sqrt{5}}\end{bmatrix} \\  & =\frac{1}{2}\begin{bmatrix}0 \\ -\frac{2}{\sqrt{5}} \\ -\frac{4}{\sqrt{5}}\end{bmatrix} \\  & =\begin{bmatrix}0 \\ -\frac{1}{\sqrt{5}} \\ -\frac{2}{\sqrt{5}}\end{bmatrix}\end{aligned}
$$

Since we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the third column of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find a vector 

$$
{u}_{3}=\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\ {x}_{2} \\ {x}_{3}\end{bmatrix}
$$

such that 

$$
\{\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\cdot {u}_{3}=0 \\ {u}_{2}\cdot {u}_{3}=0,\end{aligned}\,‖{u}_{3}‖=1.
$$

Solving this system, which is equivalent to

$$
\{\begin{aligned}\frac{2}{\sqrt{5}}{x}_{2}-\frac{1}{\sqrt{5}}{x}_{3}=0 \\ -\frac{1}{\sqrt{5}}{x}_{2}-\frac{2}{\sqrt{5}}{x}_{3}=0\end{aligned}\,⟹\,\{\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=0 \\ -{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=0,\end{aligned}
$$

we get the nonzero normalized solution 

$$
{u}_{3}=\begin{bmatrix}1 \\ 0 \\ 0\end{bmatrix}.
$$

Finally, we construct the matrix $U:$

$$
U=\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 \\ \frac{2}{\sqrt{5}} & -\frac{1}{\sqrt{5}} & 0 \\ -\frac{1}{\sqrt{5}} & -\frac{2}{\sqrt{5}} & 0\end{bmatrix}
$$"} {:db/id 17592186162725 :question/id #uuid "dcd0e8b8-71e5-5dcd-a370-0847d15e9434" :question/math-academy-id "q-98797"} {:db/id 17592186371422 :question/id #uuid "fd52405b-2747-5b7b-9ac8-728557a18405" :question/math-academy-id "q-98769" :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65acaa42-33cd-5757-90f9-4d51036d283f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\begin{bmatrix}\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} \\ \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} \\ 0\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186371424} {:answer/id #uuid "c9e77905-5ec8-56d5-8779-68fa5b781883" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\begin{bmatrix}1 \\ 0 \\ 0\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186371425} {:answer/id #uuid "97daab37-f8ae-5701-b85b-b2e30894a4f3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\begin{bmatrix}0 \\ -\frac{1}{\sqrt{5}} \\ \frac{2}{\sqrt{5}}\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186371426} {:answer/id #uuid "b4e0b9ef-caf7-5749-a8a6-77538e234c6a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\begin{bmatrix}0 \\ 0 \\ 1\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186371427} {:answer/id #uuid "a5fd5a22-26d6-55d8-a7db-98cfc7d4ab6e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\begin{bmatrix}\frac{2}{\sqrt{5}} \\ \frac{1}{\sqrt{5}} \\ 0\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186371428}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id 17592186371426} :answer-field/id #uuid "89ed90b3-a22c-5487-9e5f-dfdcd163748b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493} :db/id 17592186371423}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487} :question/problem "$$
