:db/id 17592186430275} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc8e70a8-daaa-5f24-ad61-245827641b0b" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045494} :db/id 17592186430355} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6548d6b5-487d-54bf-ae20-579bcc0c1f15" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}_{1}" :db/id 17592186430288} {:answer/id #uuid "95c3ac78-d00e-59a3-84fc-7c652182e808" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{x}_{1}" :db/id 17592186430303} {:answer/id #uuid "c2c5b684-52c7-5af3-b745-dc21f72e1303" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}_{2}" :db/id 17592186430321}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id 17592186430288} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc6db4d2-ea1d-5421-a0db-e24e2e0720aa" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045494} :db/id 17592186430358}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485} :question/problem "$$
A=\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\ 2 & 2 \\ 1 & 1\end{bmatrix},\,Σ=\begin{bmatrix}2\sqrt{3} & 0 \\ 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 0\end{bmatrix},\,V=\begin{bmatrix}\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} & -\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} \\ \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} & \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\end{bmatrix}
$$

Consider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$  select the correct options in the computations of the matrix $U$ below.

From the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce the singular values ${\sigma }_{1}=2\sqrt{3}$ and ${\sigma }_{2}=0.$

Now, we compute the $1$st column of $U.$ It is given by:

|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| ${u}_{1}=\,\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |  |
|  |  | $\frac{1}{\sqrt{6}}$ |  |  |

Since the second singular value is ${\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal.

Hence, we must find vectors $u=[{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}]^{T}$ such that ${u}_{1}\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to

$$
{{field-3}}.
$$

Choosing ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ as free variables, the general solution is given by:

|  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
|  |  | ${x}_{2}$ |  |  |
|  |  | {{field-5}} |  |  |

that we can rewrite as:

$$
\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\ {x}_{2} \\ -{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}\end{bmatrix}={x}_{1}{{\begin{bmatrix}1 \\ 0 \\ -1\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{x}_{2}{{\begin{bmatrix}0 \\ 1 \\ -2\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}}.
$$

Next, we orthogonalize ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process to get the corresponding orthogonal vectors ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$

Let ${c}_{1}={b}_{1},$ then:

|  |  | {{field-6}} |  |  |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| ${c}_{2}=\,\,$ |  | $1$ |  |  |
|  |  | {{field-7}} |  |  |

Finally, normalizing orthogonal vectors ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ we obtain the second and third columns of $U:$   

