98} :answer/value "-{x}_{1}" :db/id 17592186430303} {:answer/id #uuid "c2c5b684-52c7-5af3-b745-dc21f72e1303" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}_{2}" :db/id 17592186430321}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id 17592186430288} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc6db4d2-ea1d-5421-a0db-e24e2e0720aa" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045494} :db/id 17592186430358}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0ba1751d-87a5-51c1-9c47-8e0f1eb46649" :question/math-academy-id "q-363846" :question/problem "$$
A=\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\ 2 & 2 \\ 1 & 1\end{bmatrix},\,Σ=\begin{bmatrix}2\sqrt{3} & 0 \\ 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 0\end{bmatrix},\,V=\begin{bmatrix}\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} & -\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} \\ \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} & \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\end{bmatrix}
$$

Consider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$  select the correct options in the computations of the matrix $U$ below.

From the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce the singular values ${\sigma }_{1}=2\sqrt{3}$ and ${\sigma }_{2}=0.$

Now, we compute the $1$st column of $U.$ It is given by:

