$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "444d88e7-62f3-5333-98ad-9ee9e22d6a46" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7730 :tutorial/title "Deriving the Change of Variables Formula Using Geometry"} {:activity/course {:db/id 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id 17592186496371} :activity/id #uuid "f3fc645b-e16d-5d6f-90ed-54d6d3b27ea8" :activity/scope {:db/id 17592186050390 :topic/id #uuid "4543eb7d-4646-5b7f-9bb9-14d0a8526a45" :topic/math-academy-id 2060} :activity/steps [{:db/id 17592186496352 :step/content {:db/id 17592186496307 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0782d6f6-9606-57ec-9b7b-5f331ab47d02" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\frac{36\pi }{5}" :db/id 17592186495529} {:answer/id #uuid "844ba137-ce42-54c2-ad73-0a6795c8a9ff" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\frac{128\pi }{15}" :db/id 17592186495794} {:answer/id #uuid "d2e1bc82-3dca-5d12-a26c-e853c58acc81" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\frac{256\pi }{81}" :db/id 17592186495972} {:answer/id #uuid "d6f9f8b0-bd2d-567a-a334-e4d0096c528c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\frac{81\pi }{8}" :db/id 17592186495983} {:answer/id #uuid "f5f5beda-e35c-5b36-a994-c61647e4e3cc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\frac{64\pi }{27}" :db/id 17592186496046}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id 17592186495794} :answer-field/id #uuid "452ba3e6-053b-531a-8d38-cad4a5db1e22" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493} :db/id 17592186496108}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c2868a77-a9f6-5df5-bf81-a733f20b3a9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-161765" :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral

$$
{∭}_{R}{z}^{2}\,\text{d}V
$$

over the region $R$ enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form

$$
{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\,f(\rho \operatorname{cos}⁡\theta \operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\rho \operatorname{sin}⁡\theta \operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\rho \operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\,{\rho }^{2}\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\,d\rho \,d\theta \,dϕ.
$$

If $(\rho ,\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:

$$
x=\rho \operatorname{cos}⁡\theta \operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\,y=\rho \operatorname{sin}⁡\theta \operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\,z=\rho \operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ
$$

Notice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $2$ centered at the origin. 
As a result, in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as 

$$
Δ=\{(\rho ,\theta ,ϕ)\,:\,0\le \rho \le 2,\,0\le \theta \le 2\pi ,\,0\le ϕ\le \pi \}.
$$

Next, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)={z}^{2}:$

$$
\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\rho \operatorname{cos}⁡\theta \operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\rho \operatorname{sin}⁡\theta \operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\rho \operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\  & =(\rho \operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\  & ={\rho }^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ\end{aligned}
$$

Therefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain

$$
