alue "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(1,0)$" :db/id 17592186350595}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c882bd9a-b5e1-53bc-ab01-c27aef9ae89e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(0,0)$" :db/id 17592186350210} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6d87167-b609-5f30-9204-abd258af2b5c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186351112}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/problem "Find and categorize all local the extrema of the function $f(x,y)={e}^{x}-x+{y}^{2},$ and determine which of the following statements is true." :question/worked-solution "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.

The second partial derivative test states that if $a\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:

- If $H(a)>0,$ then:

- if $\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$

- if $\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$

- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$

- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.

First, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:

$$
\begin{aligned}f′(x,y) & =\begin{bmatrix}\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \frac{∂f}{∂y}\end{bmatrix} \\  & =\begin{bmatrix}\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x}-x+{y}^{2}) & \frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x}-x+{y}^{2})\end{bmatrix} \\  & =\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{x}-1 & 2y\end{bmatrix}\end{aligned}
$$

The first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting $f′(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:

$$
{\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}-1=0 \\ 2y=0.\end{aligned}
$$

Solving the first equation for $x$, we get

$$
{e}^{x}-1=0\,⟹\,x=0.
$$

Solving the second equation for $y,$ we get

$$
2y=0\,⟹\,y=0.
$$

Hence, $(0,0)$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y).$  

To classify this stationary point, we use the second partial derivative test.

First, we compute the second partial derivatives:

$$
\begin{aligned}\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x}-1)={e}^{x}\, & \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y)=0 \\ \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x}-1)=0\, & \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y)=2\end{aligned}
$$

So, the second derivative  is

$$
\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\begin{bmatrix}\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\ \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\end{bmatrix}=\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{x} & 0 \\ 0 & 2\end{bmatrix}.\end{aligned}
$$

Evaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain

$$
\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\ 0 & 2\end{bmatrix}.\end{aligned}
$$

Computing the determinant $H(0,0),$ we get

$$
H(0,0)=det({f}^{″}(0,0))=1\cdot 2-{0}^{2}=2>0.
$$

Finally, notice that 

$$
\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(0,0)=({f}^{″}(0,0))_{11}=1>0.
$$

Therefore, applying the second partial derivative test, we have that $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(0,0).$"} {:db/id 17592186351245 :question/id #uuid "ee951228-2bde-5532-9c92-cd8f867b2b2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-81167" :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124655 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "61cadd46-5fae-51c6-8e5b-8114e48b12da" :knowledge-point/title "Finding and Classifying All Critical Points Using the Second Partial Derivatives Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b98d01b-612c-5ed2-ab6c-3b61169ae757" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(1,-1)$" :db/id 17592186349139} {:answer/id #uuid "5ddcfc4f-27c5-55c4-ba9c-c1904e0e2050" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,0)$" :db/id 17592186349309} {:answer/id #uuid "7f92b2d4-abd6-5a84-8fd0-d31b55f917b8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(0,0)$" :db/id 17592186349619} {:answer/id #uuid "aca6c54b-336c-50d0-ab3a-ecba1f3352d4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(1,-1)$" :db/id 17592186349977} {:answer/id #uuid "d43d1028-d984-5010-bd02-d37d3bcc5bcf" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(0,0)$" :db/id 17592186350309}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d43d1028-d984-5010-bd02-d37d3bcc5bcf" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(0,0)$" :db/id 17592186350309} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a680eee-1a4f-5a68-8eb3-d37da5296409" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186350885}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/problem "Find and categorize all local the extrema of the function $f(x,y)={e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},$ and determine which of the following statements is true." :question/worked-solution "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.

The second partial derivative test states that if $a\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:

- If $H(a)>0,$ then:

- if $\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$

- if $\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$

- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$

- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.

First, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:

$$
\begin{aligned}f′(x,y) & =\begin{bmatrix}\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \frac{∂f}{∂y}\end{bmatrix} \\  & =\begin{bmatrix}\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}) & \frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})\end{bmatrix} \\  & =\begin{bmatrix}2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & 2y{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\end{bmatrix}\end{aligned}
$$

The first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting $f′(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:

$$
{\begin{aligned}2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=0 \\ 2y{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=0\end{aligned}
$$

Solving the first equation for $x,$ we get

$$
2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=0\,⟹\,x=0.
$$

Solving the second equation for $y,$ we get

$$
2y{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=0\,⟹\,y=0.
$$

Hence, $(0,0)$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y).$

To classify this stationary point, we use the second partial derivative test.

First, we compute the second partial derivatives:

$$
\begin{aligned}\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=(4{x}^{2}+2){e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\, & \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=4xy{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\ \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=4xy{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\, & \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=(4{y}^{2}+2){e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\end{aligned}
$$

So, the second derivative  is

$$
\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\begin{bmatrix}\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\ \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\end{bmatrix}=\begin{bmatrix}(4{x}^{2}+2){e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & 4xy{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\ 4xy{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & (4{y}^{2}+2){e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\end{bmatrix}.\end{aligned}
$$

Evaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain

$$
\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\ 0 & 2\end{bmatrix}.\end{aligned}
$$

Computing the determinant $H(0,0),$ we get

$$
H(0,0)=det({f}^{″}(0,0))=2\cdot 2-{0}^{2}=4>0.
$$

Finally, notice that 

$$
\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(0,0)=({f}^{″}(0,0))_{11}=2>0.
$$

Therefore, applying the second partial derivative test, we have that $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(0,0).$"} {:db/id 17592186364688 :question/id #uuid "265e39ea-f8c9-5146-9ac4-7988205838c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-105284" :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124655 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "61cadd46-5fae-51c6-8e5b-8114e48b12da" :knowledge-point/title "Finding and Classifying All Critical Points Using the Second Partial Derivatives Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e870df35-a29a-5740-b1a9-fc27a59659f0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(1,2)$" :db/id 17592186364690} {:answer/id #uuid "3dc15fc6-ed64-52d5-ac6c-18f4a5d6f76d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(2,0)$" :db/id 17592186364691} {:answer/id #uuid "e5d211e6-d042-5039-a26f-2a692ad7c3f1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(2,0)$" :db/id 17592186364692} {:answer/id #uuid "ffcc138d-043f-51f3-9bd9-aad344676fad" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(1,2)$" :db/id 17592186364693} {:answer/id #uuid "1188a71b-6d30-5d7c-b671-eeb3e30204cb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(2,0)$" :db/id 17592186364694}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5d211e6-d042-5039-a26f-2a692ad7c3f1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(2,0)$" :db/id 17592186364692} :answer-field/id #uuid "a892e43b-67a8-5cd6-aed3-b41a3dd2b871" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186364689}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/problem "Find and categorize all local the extrema of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}-4x+4,$ and determine which of the following statements is true." :question/worked-solution "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.

The second partial derivative test states that if $a\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:

- If $H(a)>0,$ then:

- if $\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$

- if $\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$

- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$

- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.

First, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:

$$
\begin{aligned}f′(x,y) & =\begin{bmatrix}\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \frac{∂f}{∂y}\end{bmatrix} \\  & =\begin{bmatrix}\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}-4x+4) & \frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}-4x+4)\end{bmatrix} \\  & =\begin{bmatrix}2x-4 & 6y\end{bmatrix}\end{aligned}
$$

The first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting $f′(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:

$$
{\begin{aligned}2x-4=0 \\ 6y=0\end{aligned}
$$

Solving the first equation for $x,$ we get

$$
2x-4=0\,⟹\,x=2.
$$

Solving the second equation for $y,$ we get

$$
6y=0\,⟹\,y=0.
$$

Hence, $(2,0)$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y).$

To classify this stationary point, we use the second partial derivative test.

First, we compute the second partial derivatives:

$$
\begin{aligned}\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x-4)=2\, & \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\frac{∂}{∂x}(6y)=0 \\ \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x-4)=0\, & \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\frac{∂}{∂y}(6y)=6\end{aligned}
$$

So, the second derivative  is

$$
\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\begin{bmatrix}\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\ \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\end{bmatrix}=\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\ 0 & 6\end{bmatrix}.\end{aligned}
$$

Evaluating the second derivative at $(2,0),$ we obtain

$$
\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(2,0)=\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\ 0 & 6\end{bmatrix}.\end{aligned}
$$

Computing the determinant $H(2,0),$ we get

$$
H(2,0)=det({f}^{″}(2,0))=2\cdot 6-{0}^{2}=12>0.
$$

Finally, notice that 

$$
\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(2,0)=({f}^{″}(2,0))_{11}=2>0.
$$

Therefore, applying the second partial derivative test, we have that $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(2,0).$"} {:db/id 17592186366372 :question/id #uuid "16f8ddbb-3563-5450-8b85-07ba89da4f50" :question/math-academy-id "q-81041" :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124655 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "61cadd46-5fae-51c6-8e5b-8114e48b12da" :knowledge-point/title "Finding and Classifying All Critical Points Using the Second Partial Derivatives Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c8732e6c-4d18-5b7e-ae95-e1ee81edf795" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(-1,0)$ and a local minimum at $(0,1)$" :db/id 17592186366374} {:answer/id #uuid "d80b2441-9615-52b5-9198-59bcb8ab0335" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(0,0)$ and a local minimum at $(1,-1)$" :db/id 17592186366375} {:answer/id #uuid "e22ffbf0-37ac-5f34-8fd8-393118213c26" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(0,0)$ and a saddle point at $(1,1)$" :db/id 17592186366376} {:answer/id #uuid "73b12864-fe9a-5b7f-9384-e568eee33a9f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(0,0)$" :db/id 17592186366377} {:answer/id #uuid "df913330-c7ab-564e-8548-6b93fa1857a8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,-1)$ and a local minimum at $(0,1)$" :db/id 17592186366378}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df913330-c7ab-564e-8548-6b93fa1857a8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,-1)$ and a local minimum at $(0,1)$" :db/id 17592186366378} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0661b48-e21e-53e3-ab77-d168dba34a8c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186366373}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/problem "Find and categorize all the local extrema of the function 
$f(x,y)$ 
given that

$$
{f}^{′}(x,y)=\begin{bmatrix}4x & 2{y}^{2}-2\end{bmatrix}.
$$" :question/worked-solution "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.

The second partial derivative test states that if $a\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:

- If $H(a)>0,$ then:

- if $\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$

- if $\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$

- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$

- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.

First, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=\begin{bmatrix}4x & 2{y}^{2}-2\end{bmatrix},$ we must have 

$$
\frac{∂f}{∂x}=4x,\,\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2{y}^{2}-2.
$$

The first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:

$$
{\begin{aligned}4x=0 \\ 2{y}^{2}=2\end{aligned}\,⟹\,{\begin{aligned}x=0 \\ y=\pm 1\end{aligned}
$$

Hence, $(0,1)$ and $(0,-1)$ are the stationary points of $f(x,y).$

To classify these stationary points, we use the second partial derivative test.

First, we compute the second partial derivatives:

$$
\begin{aligned}\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x)=4\, & \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{y}^{2}-2)=0 \\ \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x)=0\, & \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{y}^{2}-2)=4y\end{aligned}
$$

So, the second derivative is 

$$
\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\begin{bmatrix}\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\ \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\end{bmatrix}=\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\ 0 & 4y\end{bmatrix}.\end{aligned}
$$

Finally, we evaluate the second derivative at each of the stationary points and use it to categorize them:

- Evaluating the second derivative at $(0,1),$ we obtain

$$
\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,1)=\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\ 0 & 4\end{bmatrix}.\end{aligned}
$$

Computing the determinant $H(0,1),$ we get

$$
det(H(0,1))=4\cdot 4-{0}^{2}=16>0.
$$

Now, since $H(0,1)>0$ and $\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(0,1)=({f}^{″}(0,1))_{11}=4>0,$ the function $f$ has a local minimum at $(0,1).$

- Evaluating the second derivative at $(0,-1),$ we obtain

$$
\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,-1)=\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\ 0 & -4\end{bmatrix}.\end{aligned}
$$

Computing the determinant of $H(0,-1),$ we get

$$
det(H(-1,0))=4\cdot (-4)-{0}^{2}=-16<0
$$

Therefore, $f(0,-1)$ is saddle point.

In conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,-1)$ and a local minimum at $(0,1).$"} {:db/id 17592186399468 :question/id #uuid "7f004a40-028a-581c-ab25-3ac8bd7f4fe7" :question/math-academy-id "q-105281"} {:db/id 17592186399475 :question/id #uuid "e1b5f6e8-a9c9-5be3-86f7-77f082bfcf04" :question/math-academy-id "q-105338"} {:db/id 17592186434981 :question/id #uuid "05076731-f2b5-5426-b032-1f0962af8ee1" :question/math-academy-id "q-105278"} {:db/id 17592186434982 :question/id #uuid "0a336a18-c506-59a2-baef-25499733a8d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-81179"} {:db/id 17592186048450} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62cbcaa0-0a4b-5073-ad88-c02a4ba1a019" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id 17592186451299} {:answer/id #uuid "375e98ca-c238-53ff-beba-897b64cc63a8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id 17592186451300}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62cbcaa0-0a4b-5073-ad88-c02a4ba1a019" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id 17592186451299} :answer-field/id #uuid "652213cb-11aa-5dea-a4bc-3b9c8b1c355c" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id 17592186452497} {:answer/id #uuid "62cbcaa0-0a4b-5073-ad88-c02a4ba1a019" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id 17592186451299} {:answer/id #uuid "375e98ca-c238-53ff-beba-897b64cc63a8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id 17592186451300} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b0d48c5-2f2c-5055-908e-0dc95f5f7282" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id 17592186451301} {:answer/id #uuid "61b6f0c7-25e4-5be3-b321-0d0f90827d6a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id 175