| $\,-0.3\,$ | $\,0.3745\,$ | $\,0.3707\,$ | $\,0.3669\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a normal population, where 

$$
{X}_{i}∼N(\mu ,{\sigma }^{2}),\,1\le i\le n,
$$

then the sample mean $\overset{―}{X}$ has the distribution

$$
\overset{―}{X}∼N(\mu ,\frac{{\sigma }^{2}}{n}).
$$

Therefore, the distribution of the sample mean $\overset{―}{X}$ is

$$
\begin{aligned}\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\,(-14,\frac{{16}^{2}}{64}) \\  & ∼N(-14,4).\end{aligned}
$$

We're required to find $P(\overset{―}{X}>-14.86).$

First, we transform $X$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:

$$
\begin{aligned}P(\overset{―}{X}>-14.86) & =P(Z>\frac{-14.86-(-14)}{\sqrt{4}}) \\  & =P(Z>-0.43).\end{aligned}
$$

Using the complement of the normal distribution, we have

$$
\begin{aligned}P(Z>-0.43) & =1-P(Z\le -0.43) \\  & =1-Φ(-0.43).\end{aligned}
$$

From the table, we have

$$
\begin{aligned}Φ(-0.43)=0.3336.\end{aligned}
$$

Therefore, 

$$
\begin{aligned}P(\overset{―}{X}>-14.86) & =1-Φ(-0.43) \\  & =1-0.3336 \\  & =0.6664.\end{aligned}
$$"} {:db/id 17592186444049 :question/id #uuid "befb2dbc-22e5-50b6-b20b-e66d941203a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-143283" :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "522b88e5-39fe-5354-9e92-2e011cc734e7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.3745" :db/id 17592186444022} {:answer/id #uuid "58c95314-1f8b-5e7c-adb4-6319f68dbc1a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.4168" :db/id 17592186444024} {:answer/id #uuid "972153a3-5d92-518b-8c7c-7f399224d098" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.4090" :db/id 17592186444031} {:answer/id #uuid "f0eec853-9d90-50a4-a65b-1dadff4db148" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.4129" :db/id 17592186444036} {:answer/id #uuid "f6cb72ba-9e2c-53af-86f2-0dfc35913a7d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.4483" :db/id 17592186444038}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id 17592186444022} :answer-field/id #uuid "0393a2ea-4764-5c90-ab20-be9db64dea06" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493} :db/id 17592186444040}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487} :question/problem "Given that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{40}∼N(12,{20}^{2})$ is a random sample of size $40$ from a normal population, calculate $P(\overset{―}{X}<11).$

