-21865"} {:db/id 17592186049995} {:db/id 17592186167292 :question/id #uuid "66845167-3565-541d-84d8-7b2d1bbcac26" :question/math-academy-id "q-342496" :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1800471e-9188-5bb1-9aed-d377de1f651c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id 17592186315257}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id 17592186315257} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce831323-8c63-59e9-8d6f-c9bf55f74094" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045492} :db/id 17592186317035}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485} :question/problem "$$
F(x)=\{\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}, & x\in [-3,0] \\ -{x}^{2}, & x\in (0,3)\end{aligned}
$$

Consider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is converging when $x=2.$

The Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x=2.$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series

$$
S(x)=\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\sum }_{n=1}^{\infty }{a}_{n}\operatorname{cos}⁡(\frac{n\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\operatorname{sin}⁡(\frac{n\pi }{L}x).
$$

Then, the series converges for all $x\in R.$

- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:

$$
S(x)=f(x)
$$

- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:

$$
S(x)=\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}
$$

where ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{-}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.

Notice that the $6$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is continuous at $x=2.$ 

Therefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to 

$$
f(2)=-{2}^{2}=-4.
$$"} {:db/id 17592186167293 :question/id #uuid "ddf2fa7b-cdfe-5039-9760-76db03b3d4cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-342505" :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186166960 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f9dcbb0-d943-5340-916e-d789b6ab03bc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Value to What a Fourier Series Converges"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "790c545a-a448-5b09-a252-ea7895e22766" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id 17592186164979}] :question/problem "$F(x) = {0,\begin{vmatrix}x ∈ [- π, 0] \\ 1,\end{vmatrix}x ∈ (0, π)$
Consider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $2π$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ converges when $x = π$.The Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x = π$."} {:db/id 17592186317392 :question/id #uuid "883d77a7-ed55-5648-ad28-2c6a5e33fb4b" :question/math-academy-id "q-343359"} {:db/id 17592186317393 :question/id #uuid "97ff3547-7e44-5c45-a91b-58643d43344d" :question/math-academy-id "q-343364"} {:db/id 17592186317394 :question/id #uuid "086a93d6-9edf-5e6c-82a6-6ae6a865d444" :question/math-academy-id "q-343370"} {:db/id 17592186317395 :question/id #uuid "6d4ef002-5026-5196-8b56-541878d83ddc" :question/math-academy-id "q-343372" :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a90ff20b-46bc-563c-8a6d-2698d67747fc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id 17592186316068}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id 17592186316068} :answer-field/id #uuid "49330cc6-9572-5a0d-b1f2-3e73f8b2ef51" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045492} :db/id 17592186316717}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485} :question/problem "$$
F(x)=\{\begin{aligned}-2x+1, & x\in [-4,0] \\ {x}^{2}+2, & x\in (0,4)\end{aligned}
