What is the smallest possible number of solutions to the equation $g(x)=-2?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $g$ is a continuous function on $[-2,2]$, we can apply the intermediate value theorem (IVT) in any subinterval of $[-2,2].$

So, we will go through each subinterval of $[-2,2]$ and check if the IVT guarantees a solution to the equation $g(x)=-2$ on this interval.

- On the subinterval $[{\color{blue}-2},{\color{blue}-1}],$ we have $g({\color{blue}-2})={\color{red}4}$ and $g({\color{blue}-1})={\color{red}-1}.$ Since $-2 \not \in [{\color{red}-1},{\color{red}4}],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=-2$ on this subinterval.

- On the subinterval $[{\color{blue}-1},{\color{blue}0}],$ we have $g({\color{blue}-1})={\color{red}-1}$ and $g({\color{blue}0})={\color{red}-3}.$ Since $-2 \in [{\color{red}-3},{\color{red}-1}],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=-2$ on this subinterval.

- On the subinterval $[{\color{blue}0},{\color{blue}1}],$ we have $g({\color{blue}0})={\color{red}-3}$ and $g({\color{blue}1})={\color{red}5}.$ Since $-2 \in [{\color{red}-3},{\color{red}5}],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=-2$ on this subinterval.

- On the subinterval $[{\color{blue}1},{\color{blue}2}],$ we have $g({\color{blue}1})={\color{red}5}$ and $g({\color{blue}2})={\color{red}3}.$ Since $-2 \not \in[{\color{red}3},{\color{red}5}],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=-2$ on this subinterval.

The IVT guarantees solutions to $g(x)=-2$ on the subintervals $[-1,0]$ and $[0,1].$ Therefore, $g(x)=-2$ has at least two solutions."} {:db/id 17592186134470 :question/id #uuid "cca96511-88f3-5709-a640-4a289ee27d68" :question/math-academy-id "q-47011"} {:db/id 17592186134516 :question/id #uuid "f07457fa-9cf4-5158-9067-a77b8c58ade9" :question/math-academy-id "q-78457"} {:db/id 17592186317540 :question/id #uuid "9f368f3a-06db-598d-8b7b-f68ccf3faca3" :question/math-academy-id "q-344761" :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133956 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dffa987-022c-52a2-bc21-cdace7455eab" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Number of Solutions Given a Table of Values of a Continuous Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "033434ee-d7b1-53a9-b9fc-d349d23e00e5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id 17592186315135}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "033434ee-d7b1-53a9-b9fc-d349d23e00e5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id 17592186315135} :answer-field/id #uuid "3664d67e-9a2f-5ba7-8e3c-e75c2710d2ff" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id 17592186316675}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/problem "The function $p(x)$ is continuous over $x\in [-6,5].$ Some values of $p(x)$ are given in the table below. 

