What is the smallest possible number of solutions to the equation $g(x)=1?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $g$ is a continuous function on $[-4,2]$, we can apply the intermediate value theorem (IVT) in any subinterval of $[-4,2].$

So, we will go through each subinterval of $[-4,2]$ and check if the IVT guarantees a solution to the equation $g(x)=1.$

- On the subinterval $[-4,-3],$ we have $g(-4)=2$ and $g(-3)=-1.$ Since $1\in [-1,2],$  the IVT *does * guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=1$ on this subinterval.

- On the subinterval $[-3,-1],$ we have $g(-3)=-1$ and $g(-1)=-5.$ Since $1\notin [-5,-1],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=1$ on this subinterval.

- On the subinterval $[-1,0],$ we have $g(-1)=-5$ and $g(0)=2.$ Since $1\in [-5,2],$ the IVT *does * guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=1$ on this subinterval.

- On the subinterval $[0,1],$ we have $g(0)=2$ and $g(1)=3.$ Since $1\notin [2,3],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=1$ on this subinterval.

- On the subinterval $[1,2],$ we have $g(1)=3$ and $g(2)=0.$ Since $1\in [0,3],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=1$ on this subinterval.

The IVT guarantees solutions to $g(x)=1$ on the subintervals $[-4,-3],$ $[-1,0],$  and $[1,2].$ Therefore, $g(x)=1$ has at least three solutions on $[-4,2].$"} {:db/id 17592186348091 :question/id #uuid "608d1a33-0cc2-5290-bdb0-027244a4d8ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-78537"} {:db/id 17592186348092 :question/id #uuid "ae2fb37c-3d07-5853-b99d-9e361dcd461b" :question/math-academy-id "q-78542" :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133956 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dffa987-022c-52a2-bc21-cdace7455eab" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Number of Solutions Given a Table of Values of a Continuous Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a513028-7494-52ef-ad77-c476ad1110db" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id 17592186345582} {:answer/id #uuid "4f653602-ed65-5294-8d6c-a6ef40f95a14" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id 17592186346030} {:answer/id #uuid "5f729d70-c219-5012-b6a4-33000a396e60" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id 17592186346175} {:answer/id #uuid "7de2999b-8a5b-506d-ad8d-ee40e381570c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id 17592186346453} {:answer/id #uuid "c5ab31d1-2bed-5320-91a1-aa82ac71d5a5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id 17592186347069}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7de2999b-8a5b-506d-ad8d-ee40e381570c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id 17592186346453} :answer-field/id #uuid "01a7db5c-f3b0-55a6-bffd-8b68714c8c17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186347608}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/problem "The function $g(x)$ is continuous over $x\in [-1,2].$ Some values of $g(x)$ are given in the table below. 

