- On the subinterval $[-1,1],$ we have $g(-1)=2$ and $g(1)=0.$ Since $1\in [0,2],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=1$ on this subinterval.

- On the subinterval $[1,2],$ we have $g(1)=0$ and $g(2)=3.$ Since $1\in [0,3],$ the IVT  *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=1$ on this subinterval.

The IVT guarantees solutions to $g(x)=1$ on the subintervals $[-1,1]$ and $[1,2].$ Therefore, $g(x)=1$ has at least two solutions."} {:db/id 17592186399489 :question/id #uuid "07c30a16-0504-5c16-ba6b-3435983938c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-105701"} {:db/id 17592186133957 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186133481 :question/id #uuid "89750383-d5a6-5bf4-b6b2-abd489e44d3c" :question/math-academy-id "e-116"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1012ae9a-26d6-50bf-a46a-7c859d856752" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id 17592186051076}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186134472 :question/id #uuid "7de45808-287e-58b6-b2fd-45e80ad721a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-47019"} {:db/id 17592186134473 :question/id #uuid "ab400299-0ed6-5226-8178-dd355229bdff" :question/math-academy-id "q-47054"} {:db/id 17592186241118 :question/id #uuid "12db1f60-25f5-507f-bdcf-3245dfde49c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-105710"} {:db/id 17592186329800 :question/id #uuid "09a3da32-3de0-5834-befe-e2cd86cc2a45" :question/math-academy-id "q-47016"} {:db/id 17592186329801 :question/id #uuid "2fde248b-aec3-5f7a-8ce9-4a20cf4e8153" :question/math-academy-id "q-47035"} {:db/id 17592186329803 :question/id #uuid "ee052f2c-7f8b-5e1c-a710-1b1e4c2963cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-47055"} {:db/id 17592186398834 :question/id #uuid "4f66078a-9fc7-5c7e-ae29-7151d1232eff" :question/math-academy-id "q-47077"}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Intervals On Which the IVT Can Be Applied"} {:db/id 17592186133481 :question/id #uuid "89750383-d5a6-5bf4-b6b2-abd489e44d3c" :question/math-academy-id "e-116" :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id 17592186133957 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1012ae9a-26d6-50bf-a46a-7c859d856752" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Intervals On Which the IVT Can Be Applied"}] :question/problem "On which type of closed intervals can  the intermediate value theorem be applied to the function 

$$
f(x)= \dfrac{x^2+1}{x^2-1} \, ?
$$" :question/worked-solution "The intermediate value theorem can be applied to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.

The function is discontinuous at the values of $x$ which make the denominator equal to zero.

Setting the denominator equal to zero, we have $x^2-1=0,$ which corresponds to $x=1$ or $x=-1.$ 

We conclude that the intermediate value theorem can be applied to the function $f(x)$ only on closed intervals that do not contain points $x=1$ or $x=-1.$ 

