olving problems with angles, it is helpful to remember the following classifications:

- a **null** angle has a measure of $0^\circ,$

- an **acute** angle has a measure between $0^\circ$ and $90^\circ,$

- a **right** angle has a measure of $90^\circ,$

- an **obtuse** angle has a measure between $90^\circ$ and $180^\circ,$

- a **straight** angle has a measure of $180^\circ,$

- a **reflex** angle has a measure between $180^\circ$ and $360^\circ,$ and

- a **full** angle has a measure of $360^\circ.$

It is also helpful to remember that when two lines meet at a point, the opposite angles are called **vertical angles**. Vertical angles are congruent, and consequently they have the same measure." 17592186045417] [17592186111840 1065 2182 17592186045417] [17592186111840 1066 #uuid "282e8601-d3f4-5c34-a96b-a071a96de244" 17592186045417] [17592186111840 1067 "Introduction" 17592186045417] [17592186111922 1064 "Two angles are **supplementary** if their measures add up to $180^\circ.$

![](images/88/886f1ce19ad595b470d5ad13943b780bd5a22ee737908d9554e97bd08c9ce985.png)

These angles are supplementary because the sum of their measures is $180^\circ{:}$

$$
105^\circ +75^\circ = 180^\circ
$$

A **linear pair** is a pair of adjacent angles formed when two lines intersect. The angles shown above form a linear pair.

The **linear pair postulate** states the following:

If two angles form a linear pair, then they are supplementary.

The converse is *not* true. That is, if two angles are supplementary, they do not necessarily form a linear pair.

To see why, consider the diagram shown below:

![](images/29/29ff0dfefb0b535342f4e8060b37fb2404983fc150681bb02d18fa6e5b00f899.png)

Although the angles are supplementary (the sum of their measures is $120^\circ + 60^\circ = 180^\circ$), they do not form a linear pair of angles since they are not adjacent." 17592186045417] [17592186111922 1065 9518 17592186045417] [17592186111922 1066 #uuid "ea9626ef-f56f-5668-8f47-81a9f68c9db4" 17592186045417] [17592186111922 1067 "Introduction" 17592186045417] [17592186192742 1037 17592186048213 17592186045417] [17592186192742 1037 17592186049587 17592186045417] [17592186192742 1038 17592186193186 17592186045417] [17592186192742 1038 17592186193351 17592186045417] [17592186192742 1039 "Finding Alternate Interior Angles" 17592186045417] [17592186192742 1041 17592186192460 17592186045417] [17592186192742 1042 #uuid "d4b0977a-6bcb-5d7f-bec8-b10bff6d7e23" 17592186045417] [17592186192756 1038 17592186193185 17592186045417] [17592186192756 1038 17592186193385 17592186045417] [17592186192756 1039 "Further Solving Problems With Alternate Angles" 17592186045417] [17592186192756 1041 17592186192475 17592186045417] [17592186192756 1042 #uuid "7e1b5c9b-4be3-5d63-a757-fb0ec6091e3c" 17592186045417] [17592186192791 1038 17592186192946 17592186045417] [17592186192791 1038 17592186193187 17592186045417] [17592186192791 1039 "Finding Alternate Exterior Angles" 17592186045417] [17592186192791 1041 17592186192515 17592186045417] [17592186192791 1042 #uuid "18bf826f-de64-5905-9e7b-b87a3b4e1227" 17592186045417] [17592186192792 1038 17592186193184 17592186045417] [17592186192792 1038 17592186193386 17592186045417] [17592186192792 1039 "Solving Problems With Alternate Angles" 17592186045417] [17592186192792 1041 17592186192516 17592186045417] [17592186192792 1042 #uuid "77d962d1-2421-592e-b825-e33ebe277b67" 17592186045417] [17592186192902 1043 17592186193595 17592186045417] [17592186192902 1043 17592186193596 17592186045417] [17592186192902 1043 17592186193616 17592186045417] [17592186192902 1043 17592186193673 17592186045417] [17592186192902 1043 17592186193674 17592186045417] [17592