We have already seen how to estimate the area using left and right Riemann sums, in which we approximate the area using rectangles.

However, we can get an even better estimate using trapezoids instead. The process of approximating the area under a curve using trapezoids is called the **trapezoidal rule.**

Here, we will approximate the area under the graph of ![](images/2e/2eb046be7e52e5a72d29a1333f9cf483411f0a8d6d897fe53014cc1952c6f759.svg) with three trapezoids, each with a height equal to ![](images/da/daae2fd3251c4ba175906af980d1c33d4db057c616509ec6a146307d64417228.svg) Note that the trapezoids are turned on their side, so *their heights run horizontally*.

![](images/13/13b034759b6acf4a451b0725e6abf4d23ae1dc80e567ee792ad51116e35f3373.png)

We compute the area of each trapezoid and add them all together:

![](images/09/093f580f625b22a2cf914a85c64b433e280e30071df9c934ddef49af5034e1da.svg)

Because the function is *concave up* over the interval ![](images/85/8559262277e3f52fd1eb8139fad407ed50f80873ec56c7349969a9a3ee6789e4.svg) the trapezoids reach above the function, and therefore our approximation is an *overestimate* of the actual area." 17592186045417] [17592186093489 1065 745 17592186045417] [17592186093489 1066 #uuid "e65800fc-8bfa-5eef-910a-646db63059f3" 17592186045417] [17592186093489 1067 "Introduction" 17592186045417] [17592186093490 1064 "So far, the step size $\Delta x$ has been the same for each trapezoid. In these cases, we say that the step size is **regular**.

